) : ℂ)) • (ρ (i⁻¹ * j)) := by\n621 |     rw [hB_star]\n622 |     show (∑ g ∈ s, (c g : ℂ) • (ρ g⁻¹)) * (∑ g ∈ s, (conj (c g) : ℂ) • (ρ g)) = _\n623 |     rw [Finset.sum_mul]\n624 |     simp only [Finset.mul_sum]\n625 |     apply Finset.sum_congr rfl\n626 |     intro i hi\n627 |     apply Finset.sum_congr rfl\n628 |     intro j hj\n629 |     rw [Algebra.smul_mul_assoc, Algebra.mul_smul_comm, smul_smul, ← ρ.map_mul]\n\n... 12 more line(s) below (use offset=630 to continue reading)","timestamp":1784912735,"token_count":4438,"tool_call_id":"8eb89bb4-4d43-47c9-ac8b-e239def805af","full_token_estimate":3233,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/PositiveDefinite.lean","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":641,"byte_count":30782,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":4,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":12,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\found_issues.md\n-- 214 lines, 9612 bytes --\n 51 | - `lattice_ym_reflection_positive_periodic` theorem now requires the hypothesis.\n 52 | - The `Z4Expectation` structure (in the same file) has a similar issue but is\n 53 |   already known to be vacuous for nonempty lattices due to the `hadd` issue.\n 54 | \n 55 | ## 2. `transferMatrix_identity` in TransferMatrix.lean is incorrect\n 56 | \n 57 | ### What was wrong\n 58 | \n 59 | The lemma claimed:\n 60 | \n 61 | ```\n 62 | ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ∫ G_plus(u)·(T G_plus)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n 63 | ```\n 64 | \n 65 | where `G_plus(u) = G(U⁺, 𝟙, U⁰)` (negative links set to 1).  This is false\n 66 | because:\n 67 | \n 68 | 1. `G` depends on negative links through `f` and `S_OS_int`, while `G_plus`\n 69 |    replaces negative links with 1.\n 70 | 2. `transferMatrix_positive` shows `(T ψ)(u) = ψ(u)·(positive kernel)` because\n 71 |    `restrict_merge_id` makes `ψ(restrictPosInterface(merge(U⁻, u))) = ψ(u)`,\n 72 |    independent of U⁻.  This means T is a **multiplication operator**, not a\n 73 |    genuine transfer matrix that couples positive and negative configurations.\n 74 | \n 75 | ### Current status (2025-06-28, corrected)\n 76 | \n 77 | The corrected transfer matrix has been implemented in `TransferMatrix.lean`:\n 78 | \n 79 | - **`reflectToPosInterface`**: The map θ⁻⁰ from negative+interface to positive+interface.\n 80 |   Uses the `onePosInterface` (identity on positive links) plus U_zero on interface links,\n 81 |   then applies the full reflection and restricts to positive+interface sites.\n 82 | \n 83 | - **`g_posInterface`**: The function g(u) = f(u)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(u)/2) for u ∈ PosInterfaceConfig.\n 84 |   Depends only on positive+interface links because f does (by `hf_supported`).\n 85 | \n 86 | - **`osPositiveOfPosInterface`**: Extracts S_OS⁺(U⁺) from a positive+interface config.\n 87 | \n 88 | - **`transferMatrixCorrect`**: The correct transfer matrix with kernel\n 89 |   ```\n 90 |   (T ψ)(u) = ∫ ψ(θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰))\n 91 |               · exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(u)/2 + S_OS⁻(U⁻)/2 + S_OS_int(u, U⁻)))\n 92 |               dμ⁻(U⁻)\n 93 |   ```\n 94 | \n 95 |   The kernel is chosen so that the key identity holds:\n 96 |   ```\n 97 |   g(u)·(T g)(u) = ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ⁻(U⁻)    (for each fixed U⁺,U⁰)\n 98 |   ```\n 99 | \n100 | ### Remaining work\n101 | \n102 | 1. **`integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`**: ✅ PROVEN (0 sorries). The measure-theoretic identity\n103 |    ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ∫_{PosInt} g(u)·(T g)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n104 |    is fully proven using `measure_factorization'`, the action reflection lemmas, and the\n105 |    definitions of `transferMatrixCorrect` and `g_posInterface`.\n106 | \n107 | 2. **`transferMatrixCorrect_positive`**: ❌ BLOCKED — requires Peter-Weyl theorem for SU(N).\n108 |    See §3 below for the precise mathematical obstruction.\n109 | \n110 | 3. **`gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive`**: The final `sorry` in\n111 |    `ReflectionPositivity.lean` (line 1437) that depends on (2).\n112 | \n113 | See `docs/gap_analysis.md` and `src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean` for details.\n114 | \n115 | ## 3. Tr(gh) is NOT positive-definite on SU(N) × SU(N) — fundamental obstruction\n116 | \n117 | ### The key question\n118 | \n119 | The Osterwalder-Seiler proof requires showing that the plaquette Boltzmann factor\n120 | `exp(c · Re Tr(U₁U₂U₃U₄))` is a positive-definite function on `SU(N)^4` (the product\n121 | group of link variables around a plaquette). Since `exp(c · Re Tr(g))` is PD on `SU(N)`\n122 | (proven in `PositiveDefinite.lean`), the natural approach is to show that the composition\n123 | of a PD function with the multiplication map `m: SU(N)^4 → SU(N)` preserves PD-ness.\n124 | \n125 | This reduces to: **is `(g,h) ↦ Tr(gh)` positive-definite on `SU(N) × SU(N)`?**\n126 | \n127 | ### Counterexample: Tr(gh) is NOT PD on SU(2) × SU(2)\n128 | \n129 | Take three points in `SU(2) × SU(2)` with coefficients `c₁ = c₂ = c₃ = 1`:\n130 | - `(g₁, h₁) = (iσ₁, iσ₁)`\n131 | - `(g₂, h₂) = (iσ₂, iσ₂)`\n132 | - `(g₃, h₃) = (iσ₃, iσ₃)`\n133 | \n134 | where `iσ_k` are the Pauli matrices (elements of SU(2) with trace 0 and square -I).\n135 | \n136 | The kernel matrix `K_{ab} = Tr(g_a⁻¹ g_b h_a⁻¹ h_b)` is:\n137 | \n138 | ```\n139 | K = [[ 2, -2, -2],\n140 |      [-2,  2, -2],\n141 |      [-2, -2,  2]]\n142 | ```\n143 | \n144 | - Diagonal: `K_{aa} = Tr(I) = 2`.\n145 | - Off-diagonal: `K_{ab} = Tr((g_a⁻¹ g_b)²) = Tr(-I) = -2` (since `g_a⁻¹ g_b` is a\n146 |   Pauli matrix, whose square is `-I`).\n147 | \n148 | Eigenvalues: **-2** (eigenvector [1,1,1]), 4, 4.\n149 | \n150 | The quadratic form `∑ c_a conj(c_b) K_{ab} = c* K c = -6 < 0` for `c = [1,1,1]`.\n151 | \n152 | ### Why this matters\n153 | \n154 | This counterexample proves that:\n155 | 1. `(g,h) ↦ Tr(gh)` is **NOT** positive-definite on `SU(N) × SU(N)` for `N ≥ 2`.\n156 | 2. The multiplication map `m: G^k → G` does **NOT** preserve positive-definiteness for\n157 |    non-abelian groups (it is not a group homomorphism).\n158 | 3. The plaquette Boltzmann factor `exp(c · Re Tr(U₁U₂U₃U₄))` is **NOT** positive-definite\n159 |    on `SU(N)^4` as a function of the link variables.\n160 | 4. The transfer matrix positivity **cannot** be proven by simply composing\n161 |    `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite` with the plaquette product map.\n162 | \n163 | ### What IS true (and why Peter-Weyl is needed)\n164 | \n165 | The function `(g,h) ↦ Tr(gh⁻¹)` **IS** positive-definite on `SU(N) × SU(N)` — it is the\n166 | matrix coefficient `⟨(g ⊗ h̄)Ω, Ω⟩` of the unitary representation `π(g,h) = g ⊗ h̄`\n167 | (the tensor product of the fundamental representation and its dual).\n168 | \n169 | But `Tr(gh)` requires the \"wrong\" group action (multiplication instead of multiplication-by-\n170 | inverse), which is an **anti-representation**, not a representation. Matrix coefficients of\n171 | anti-representations are NOT positive-definite in general.\n172 | \n173 | The character expansion resolves this by decomposing:\n174 | ```\n175 | exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃g₄)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃g₄)\n176 |                          = ∑_λ a_λ ∑_{μ,ν,ρ,σ} C_{μνρσ}^λ χ_μ(g₁) χ_ν(g₂) χ_ρ(g₃) χ_σ(g₄)\n177 | ```\n178 | where `a_λ ≥ 0`, `C_{μνρσ}^λ ≥ 0` (Littlewood-Richardson coefficients), and each\n179 | `χ_μ(g₁) χ_ν(g₂) χ_ρ(g₃) χ_σ(g₄)` is PD on `SU(N)^4` (product of PD functions on\n180 | different factors). The sum of PD functions with non-negative coefficients is PD.\n181 | \n182 | **Progress (2025-06-29)**: The \"product of PD on different factors\" step has been\n183 | formalized as `PositiveDefinite.prod` in `PositiveDefinite.lean` (0 sorries).\n184 | This proves that if `φ : G → ℂ` is PD and `ψ : H → ℂ` is PD, then\n185 | `(g,h) ↦ φ(g)·ψ(h)` is PD on `G × H`. The proof uses the Schur product theorem\n186 | combined with a grouping argument (`PositiveDefinite.sum_nonneg_of_map`) that\n187 | handles non-injective index maps.\n188 | \n189 | Additionally, `repCharacter_positiveDefinite` has been proven (0 sorries): the\n190 | character `χ(g) = Tr(ρ(g))` of any unitary representation `ρ: G →* U(n)` is\n191 | positive-definite. This generalizes `fundamentalCharacter_positiveDefinite` from\n192 | the fundamental representation of `SU(N)` to arbitrary unitary representations.\n193 | The proof uses the same technique: the PD sum equals `Tr(Bᴴ * B) ≥ 0` where\n194 | `B = ∑ conj(c_g) • ρ(g)`, using `ρ(g)ᴴ = ρ(g⁻¹)` (unitary + homomorphism).\n195 | \n196 | The remaining ingredients (Peter-Weyl theorem and Clebsch-Gordan decomposition)\n197 | are still not available in Mathlib.\n198 | \n199 | **This decomposition requires the Peter-Weyl theorem, which is not in Mathlib.**\n200 | \n201 | ### Conclusion\n202 | \n203 | The sorry in `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` (ReflectionPositivity.lean:1437)\n204 | **cannot be closed** without one of:\n205 | - **Peter-Weyl theorem** for SU(N) (character expansion with non-negative coefficients)\n206 | - **Heat kernel** on SU(N) (representing `exp(c · Re Tr(g))` as Fourier transform of a\n207 |   positive measure on the Lie algebra)\n208 | - **Clebsch-Gordan decomposition** for SU(N) (decomposing characters of products)\n209 | \n210 | All of these require representation theory of compact Lie groups that goes beyond what\n211 | Mathlib currently provides. The `PositiveDefinite.lean` infrastructure (PD functions, Schur\n212 | product theorem, `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite`) is necessary but **not sufficient** — the\n213 | missing ingredient is the decomposition of `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁...gₖ))` into a sum of products\n214 | of PD functions on individual factors.\n","timestamp":1784912735,"token_count":2648,"tool_call_id":"55fd5935-768e-4632-aa66-b3c0f7fa0f77","full_token_estimate":1941,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"docs/found_issues.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":214,"byte_count":9612,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":4,"is_prune_marker":false}
