{"id":25,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\gap_analysis.md\n-- 293 lines, 14276 bytes --\n 41 | \n 42 | ### The remaining gap:\n 43 | \n 44 | $$\\int_{\\text{config}} G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0(U) \\ge 0$$\n 45 | \n 46 | where $\\mu_0$ is the product Haar measure on $\\text{SU}(N)^{\\text{links}}$.\n 47 | \n 48 | ### ✅ Key identity PROVEN (2025-06-28)\n 49 | \n 50 | The measure-theoretic identity\n 51 | $$\\int G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0(U) = \\int_{\\text{PosInt}} g(u) \\cdot (Tg)(u) \\, d\\mu^{\\pm 0}(u)$$\n 52 | is fully proven in `TransferMatrix.lean` (`integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`, 0 sorries).\n 53 | This reduces the problem to showing $\\langle g, Tg \\rangle \\ge 0$.\n 54 | \n 55 | ### ❌ Transfer matrix positivity BLOCKED (2025-06-28)\n 56 | \n 57 | The transfer matrix $T$ is positive **if and only if** its integral kernel is a\n 58 | positive-definite function on the group of link variables. The kernel is a product of\n 59 | plaquette Boltzmann factors $\\exp(c \\cdot \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(U_{\\partial p}))$.\n 60 | \n 61 | **Fundamental obstruction**: The function $(g_1, g_2, g_3, g_4) \\mapsto \\exp(c \\cdot \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g_1 g_2 g_3 g_4))$ is **NOT** positive-definite on $\\text{SU}(N)^4$ for $N \\ge 2$.\n 62 | \n 63 | This is because even the simpler function $(g, h) \\mapsto \\operatorname{Tr}(gh)$ is NOT\n 64 | positive-definite on $\\text{SU}(N) \\times \\text{SU}(N)$. A concrete counterexample for\n 65 | $N=2$ uses the Pauli matrices $i\\sigma_1, i\\sigma_2, i\\sigma_3 \\in \\text{SU}(2)$:\n 66 | \n 67 | With $(g_a, h_a) = (i\\sigma_a, i\\sigma_a)$ for $a=1,2,3$ and $c_a = 1$, the kernel matrix\n 68 | $K_{ab} = \\operatorname{Tr}(g_a^{-1} g_b h_a^{-1} h_b)$ has eigenvalues $-2, 4, 4$, giving\n 69 | $\\sum c_a \\overline{c_b} K_{ab} = -6 < 0$.\n 70 | \n 71 | See `docs/found_issues.md` §3 for the full analysis.\n 72 | \n 73 | ## Why This Is Non-Trivial\n 74 | \n 75 | The integrand $G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U)$ is **not pointwise non-negative**. The\n 76 | non-negativity holds only after integration. This is the essence of reflection\n 77 | positivity: it is a global property of the measure, not a pointwise one.\n 78 | \n 79 | ## Mathematical Proof (Osterwalder-Seiler 1979, §3)\n 80 | \n 81 | ### Step 1: Measure factorization\n 82 | \n 83 | The links are partitioned into three sets:\n 84 | - $L^+$: links with positive signed time\n 85 | - $L^-$: links with negative signed time\n 86 | - $L^0$: links at the time interface (signed time = 0)\n 87 | \n 88 | The product Haar measure factorizes:\n 89 | $$\\mu_0 = \\mu^+ \\otimes \\mu^- \\otimes \\mu^0$$\n 90 | \n 91 | ### Step 2: Transfer matrix\n 92 | \n 93 | Define the transfer matrix $T$ acting on $L^2(\\mu^+ \\otimes \\mu^0)$ by:\n 94 | \n 95 | $$(T\\psi)(U^+, U^0) = \\int_{U^-} \\psi(U^-, U^0) \\cdot K(U^+, U^-, U^0) \\, d\\mu^-(U^-)$$\n 96 | \n 97 | where $K(U^+, U^-, U^0) = \\exp(-\\beta \\cdot S^0_{OS}(U^+, U^-, U^0))$ is the\n 98 | interface Boltzmann factor.\n 99 | \n100 | ### Step 3: Key identity\n101 | \n102 | $$\\int G(U) G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0 = \\langle G^+, T G^+ \\rangle_{L^2(\\mu^+ \\otimes \\mu^0)}$$\n103 | \n104 | where $G^+$ is the restriction of $G$ to $L^+ \\cup L^0$.\n105 | \n106 | ### Step 4: Positivity of $T$\n107 | \n108 | The transfer matrix $T$ is a **positive operator** (self-adjoint with non-negative\n109 | spectrum). This follows from the Peter-Weyl theorem on $\\text{SU}(N)$:\n110 | \n111 | 1. The kernel $K(U^+, U^-, U^0)$ is a product over interface plaquettes:\n112 |    $$K = \\prod_{\\text{interface plaquettes } p} \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{N} \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(U_{\\partial p})\\right)$$\n113 | \n114 | 2. For a single plaquette, the function $\\exp(c \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g))$ on\n115 |    $\\text{SU}(N)$ can be expanded in characters (representations):\n116 |    $$\\exp(c \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g)) = \\sum_{\\lambda} a_\\lambda(c) \\chi_\\lambda(g)$$\n117 |    with $a_\\lambda(c) \\ge 0$ for $c > 0$.\n118 | \n119 | 3. The characters $\\chi_\\lambda$ are positive-definite functions on $\\text{SU}(N)$,\n120 |    and the tensor product of positive-definite functions is positive-definite.\n121 | \n122 | 4. By the Peter-Weyl theorem, the matrix elements of irreducible representations\n123 |    form an orthonormal basis of $L^2(\\text{SU}(N))$, and any positive-definite\n124 |    function expands with non-negative coefficients in this basis.\n125 | \n126 | 5. Therefore $K$ is a positive-definite kernel, $T$ is a positive operator,\n127 |    and $\\langle G^+, T G^+ \\rangle \\ge 0$.\n128 | \n129 | ## Required Mathlib Infrastructure\n130 | \n131 | ### 1. Peter-Weyl Theorem for $\\text{SU}(N)$\n132 | \n133 | **Not yet in Mathlib.** The following pieces are needed:\n134 | \n135 | | Piece | Status in Mathlib | Notes |\n136 | |-------|-------------------|-------|\n137 | | Compact Lie group $\\text{SU}(N)$ | Done (`SpecialUnitary.lean`) | Topological group, compactness, Haar measure |\n138 | | Representation theory of compact groups | Partial | `RepresentationTheory/` covers finite groups |\n139 | | Unitary irreps of $\\text{SU}(N)$ | Not available | Requires highest weight theory |\n140 | | Peter-Weyl theorem | Not available | $L^2(G) \\cong \\bigoplus_\\lambda V_\\lambda \\otimes V_\\lambda^*$ |\n\n... 153 more line(s) below (use offset=141 to continue reading)","timestamp":1784913210,"token_count":1633,"tool_call_id":"5dd45a81-2580-4354-af26-136d6eae46d3","full_token_estimate":1231,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"docs/gap_analysis.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":293,"byte_count":14276,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":15,"edit_line":null},"turn":12,"is_prune_marker":false}
