{"id":33,"role":"Assistant","content":"Let me look at the actual Osterwalder-Seiler proof structure and the transfer matrix kernel more carefully.","timestamp":1784915208,"token_count":30,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"limit\":100,\"offset\":141,\"path\":\"docs/gap_analysis.md\"}","name":"read_file"},"id":"61006f48-6a36-40b0-abe0-c3a411b579c2","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean\",\"limit\":80,\"offset\":1246}","name":"read_file"},"id":"6c502d74-489f-4323-8993-7e649ec26618","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":11948,"reasoning_content":"Now I understand the plaquette structure. The plaquette product is:\n```\nU_p = U(n,μ) * U(n+μ,ν) * U(n+μ+ν,μ)⁻¹ * U(n+ν,ν)⁻¹\n```\n\nSo it's `g₁ * g₂ * g₃⁻¹ * g₄⁻¹` — with inverses on the 3rd and 4th links! This is the standard plaquette holonomy with orientation reversal.\n\nSo the plaquette Boltzmann factor is `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))`.\n\nNow, the key mathematical fact is that `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is PD on `G⁴`. I proved above that `χ_λ(gh⁻¹)` is PD on `G × G` using the matrix coefficient expansion. For `G⁴`, we need `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`.\n\nLet me think about how to prove `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is PD on `G⁴`.\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ)`.\n\nLet `A = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` and `B = ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄)`. Then `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ABᴴ) = ⟨B, A⟩_HS`.\n\nNow, `A_{ij} = ∑_k (ρ_λ(g₁))_{ik} (ρ_λ(g₂))_{kj}` and `B_{ij} = ∑_l (ρ_λ(g₃))_{il} (ρ_λ(g₄))_{lj}`.\n\n`Tr(ABᴴ) = ∑_{i,j} A_{ij} conj(B_{ij}) = ∑_{i,j,k,l} (ρ_λ(g₁))_{ik} (ρ_λ(g₂))_{kj} conj((ρ_λ(g₃))_{il}) conj((ρ_λ(g₄))_{lj})`.\n\nFor the PD sum on `G⁴`:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) χ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ (g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)⁻¹ (g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)⁻¹)`\n\nNow, `g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ (g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)⁻¹ (g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)⁻¹ = g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ g₃ᵇ⁻¹g₃ᵃ g₄ᵇ⁻¹g₄ᵃ`.\n\nAnd `χ_λ` of this equals `Tr(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ⁻¹g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ⁻¹g₄ᵃ))`.\n\nUsing `ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ⁻¹g₃ᵃ) = ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)⁻¹ = ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴ`:\n\n`= Tr(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴ)`\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me use the approach from the `χ_λ(gh⁻¹)` proof.\n\nActually, let me use a cleaner approach. Define `Φ: G⁴ → Mat_n(ℂ)` by `Φ(g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄) = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ`. Then `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(Φ(g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄))`.\n\nFor the PD sum, we need:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) Tr(Φ(x_a⁻¹x_b))` where `x_a = (g₁ᵃ,g₂ᵃ,g₃ᵃ,g₄ᵃ)`.\n\nNow, `Φ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)ᴴ`.\n\nUsing `ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)`:\n\n`Φ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)ᴴ`\n\nAnd `ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)ᴴ = (ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵇ))ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)`.\n\nSo `Φ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)ᴴ`.\n\nAnd `ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)`.\n\nSo `Φ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)`.\n\nNow, `Tr(Φ(x_a⁻¹x_b)) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ))`.\n\nUsing the cyclic property of trace, we can rearrange. But this is getting very messy. Let me think about whether there's a cleaner way.\n\nActually, let me use the Hilbert-Schmidt inner product approach. Define:\n- `A_a = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)` (a function of `g₁ᵃ, g₂ᵃ`)\n- `B_a = ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)` (a function of `g₃ᵃ, g₄ᵃ`)\n\nThen `χ_λ(g₁ᵃg₂ᵃg₃ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵃ⁻¹) = Tr(A_a B_aᴴ) = ⟨B_a, A_a⟩_HS`.\n\nNow, `A_a` depends only on `(g₁ᵃ, g₂ᵃ)` and `B_a` depends only on `(g₃ᵃ, g₄ᵃ)`.\n\nFor the PD sum:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b)`\n\nwhere `x_a⁻¹x_b = (g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ, g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)`.\n\n`χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)⁻¹)`\n\n`= Tr(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ))`\n\nLet me define `M_a = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴ` ... no, this doesn't simplify nicely because the factors alternate between `a` and `b`.\n\nLet me try a different grouping. Let me use the fact that `Tr(PQ) = ∑_{i,j} P_{ij} Q_{ji}` and expand everything.\n\nActually, let me try the approach that worked for `G × G`. There, we had:\n`χ_λ(gh⁻¹) = ∑_{i,j} ρ_λ(g)_{ij} conj(ρ_λ(h)_{ij})`\n\nand the PD sum became `∑_{i,j} |∑_a c_a ρ_λ(g_a)_{ij} conj(something)|²`.\n\nFor `G⁴`, let me try to write `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` as a sum of products of the form `f(g₁,g₂) conj(f(g₃,g₄))` where `f` is some function. If I can do this, then the PD sum becomes a sum of `|·|²`.\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ)`\n\nLet `A = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)`, `B = ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄)`. Then `χ_λ = Tr(ABᴴ) = ⟨B, A⟩_HS = ∑_{i,j} conj(B_{ij}) A_{ij} = ∑_{i,j} A_{ij} conj(B_{ij})`.\n\nNow, `A_{ij} = ∑_k ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj}` and `B_{ij} = ∑_l ρ_λ(g₃)_{il} ρ_λ(g₄)_{lj}`.\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ∑_{i,j} (∑_k ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj}) conj(∑_l ρ_λ(g₃)_{il} ρ_λ(g₄)_{lj})`\n`= ∑_{i,j,k,l} ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj} conj(ρ_λ(g₃)_{il}) conj(ρ_λ(g₄)_{lj})`.\n\nNow, for the PD sum on `G⁴`:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b)`\n\nwhere `x_a = (g₁ᵃ, g₂ᵃ, g₃ᵃ, g₄ᵃ)` and `x_a⁻¹x_b = (g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ, g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)`.\n\n`χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ∑_{i,j,k,l} ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)_{kj} conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)_{il}) conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)_{lj})`\n\nNow, `ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)_{ik} = ∑_p (ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)⁻¹)_{ip} ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk} = ∑_p conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)_{pi}) ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk}` (unitary).\n\nSimilarly:\n- `ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)_{kj} = ∑_q conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)_{qk}) ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj}`\n- `ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)_{il} = ∑_r conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{ri}) ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl}`\n- `ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)_{lj} = ∑_s conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{sl}) ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj}`\n\nSo:\n`conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)_{il}) = ∑_r ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{ri} conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl})`\n`conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)_{lj}) = ∑_s ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{sl} conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj})`\n\nPutting it all together:\n`χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ∑_{i,j,k,l,p,q,r,s} [conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)_{pi}) ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk}] [conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)_{qk}) ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj}] [ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{ri} conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl})] [ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{sl} conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj})]`\n\nThe PD sum:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b)`\n`= ∑_{i,j,k,l,p,q,r,s} ∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) [conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)_{pi}) ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk}] [conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)_{qk}) ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj}] [ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{ri} conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl})] [ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{sl} conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj})]`\n\nNow, the `a`-dependent factors are: `conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)_{pi})`, `conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)_{qk})`, `ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{ri}`, `ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{sl})`.\nThe `b`-dependent factors are: `ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk}`, `ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj}`, `conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl})`, `conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj})`.\n\nSo the `a`-part is: `c_a · conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)_{pi}) · conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)_{qk}) · ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{ri} · ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{sl})`\nAnd the `b`-part is: `conj(c_b) · ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk} · ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj} · conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl}) · conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj})`\n\nThe `b`-part is `conj(c_b · conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk}) · conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj}) · ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl} · ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj}))`... \n\nHmm wait, let me check: `conj(c_b · ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk} · ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj} · conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl}) · conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj}))`\n`= conj(c_b) · conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk}) · conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj}) · ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl} · ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj})`.\n\nBut the `b`-part I have is: `conj(c_b) · ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk} · ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj} · conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{rl}) · conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{sj})`.\n\nThese don't match — the conj pattern is different. The `a`-part has `conj, conj, no-conj, no-conj` while the `b`-part has `no-conj, no-conj, conj, conj`. For the sum to be `|·|²`, we need the `b`-part to be the conjugate of the `a`-part (with `a` replaced by `b`).\n\nThe `a`-part: `c_a · conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{pi}) · conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{qk}) · ρ₃ᵃ_{ri} · ρ₄ᵃ_{sl})`.\nIts conjugate (with `a→b`): `conj(c_b) · ρ₁ᵇ_{pi} · ρ₂ᵇ_{qk} · conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{ri}) · conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{sl}))`.\n\nBut the `b`-part is: `conj(c_b) · ρ₁ᵇ_{pk} · ρ₂ᵇ_{qj} · conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{rl}) · conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{sj})`.\n\nThe indices don't match: `a`-part has `(pi, qk, ri, sl)` while `b`-part has `(pk, qj, rl, sj)`. These are different index patterns, so it's NOT a simple `|·|²`.\n\nHmm, so the direct matrix coefficient expansion doesn't give a clean `|·|²` for `G⁴`. Let me think about this differently.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that I need to use the cyclic property of trace more carefully. Let me use:\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ)`\n\nUsing cyclic property: `= Tr(ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ)`.\n\nNow, `Tr(ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ, ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)⟩_HS`... no, `Tr(AᴴB) = ⟨A, B⟩_HS`, so `Tr(ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ) = Tr((ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ)ᴴ ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂))`... \n\nHmm, `(ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ)ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ`. So `Tr(ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ) = Tr((ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ)ᴴ ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ, ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)⟩_HS`.\n\nWait, that's not right either. `Tr(AᴴB) = ⟨A, B⟩_HS`. So `Tr((ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ)ᴴ · ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ, ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)⟩_HS`.\n\nBut `ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ ≠ ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ` in general (matrix multiplication is not commutative).\n\nOK let me try yet another approach. The key insight is:\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂) · (ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄))ᴴ)`.\n\nWait: `(ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄))ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ = (ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄))⁻¹`. And `ρ_λ(g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹`. So `(ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄))ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ = ρ_λ(g₄⁻¹)ρ_λ(g₃⁻¹) = ρ_λ(g₄⁻¹g₃⁻¹)`.\n\nBut `g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹ ≠ g₄⁻¹g₃⁻¹` in general. So `(ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄))ᴴ ≠ ρ_λ(g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`.\n\nHowever, `ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹ = ρ_λ(g₃⁻¹)ρ_λ(g₄⁻¹) = ρ_λ(g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`. And `(ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄))ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄⁻¹)ρ_λ(g₃⁻¹) = ρ_λ(g₄⁻¹g₃⁻¹)`.\n\nSo `ρ_λ(g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹` while `(ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄))ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄⁻¹g₃⁻¹)`. These are different (order reversed).\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹))` but this is NOT `Tr(A · Bᴴ)` for `A = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)`, `B = ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄)`.\n\nHmm, but `Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹)`.\n\nAnd `Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂) · (ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃))ᴴ) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂) · ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹)`.\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(A · Cᴴ)` where `A = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` and `C = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)` (note the reversed order!).\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨C, A⟩_HS = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃), ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)⟩_HS`.\n\nNow, `⟨C, A⟩_HS = ∑_{i,j} conj(C_{ij}) A_{ij} = ∑_{i,j} A_{ij} conj(C_{ij})`.\n\n`A_{ij} = ∑_k ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj}` (function of `g₁, g₂`).\n`C_{ij} = ∑_l ρ_λ(g₄)_{il} ρ_λ(g₃)_{lj}` (function of `g₃, g₄` — note reversed order).\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ∑_{i,j} (∑_k ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj}) · conj(∑_l ρ_λ(g₄)_{il} ρ_λ(g₃)_{lj})`\n`= ∑_{i,j,k,l} ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj} conj(ρ_λ(g₄)_{il}) conj(ρ_λ(g₃)_{lj})`.\n\nNow, for the PD sum on `G⁴`:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b)`\n\nwhere `x_a⁻¹x_b = (g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ, g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)`.\n\n`χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ∑_{i,j,k,l} ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)_{kj} conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)_{il}) conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)_{lj})`\n\nExpanding each:\n- `ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)_{ik} = ∑_p conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)_{pi}) ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk}`\n- `ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)_{kj} = ∑_q conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)_{qk}) ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj}`\n- `conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)_{il}) = conj(∑_r conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{ri}) ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{rl}) = ∑_r ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{ri} conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{rl})`\n- `conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)_{lj}) = conj(∑_s conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{sl}) ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{sj}) = ∑_s ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{sl} conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{sj})`\n\nSo:\n`χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ∑_{i,j,k,l,p,q,r,s} [conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{pi}) ρ₁ᵇ_{pk}] [conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{qk}) ρ₂ᵇ_{qj}] [ρ₄ᵃ_{ri} conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{rl})] [ρ₃ᵃ_{sl} conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sj})]`\n\nThe `a`-dependent factors: `conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{pi}), conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{qk}), ρ₄ᵃ_{ri}, ρ₃ᵃ_{sl})`.\nThe `b`-dependent factors: `ρ₁ᵇ_{pk}, ρ₂ᵇ_{qj}, conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{rl}), conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sj})`.\n\nThe `a`-part (with `c_a`): `c_a · conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{pi}) · conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{qk}) · ρ₄ᵃ_{ri} · ρ₃ᵃ_{sl})`.\nThe `b`-part (with `conj(c_b)`): `conj(c_b) · ρ₁ᵇ_{pk} · ρ₂ᵇ_{qj} · conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{rl}) · conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sj})`.\n\nIs the `b`-part the conjugate of the `a`-part (with `a→b`)?\n\nConjugate of `a`-part (with `a→b`): `conj(c_b) · ρ₁ᵇ_{pi} · ρ₂ᵇ_{qk} · conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{ri}) · conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sl})`.\n\nBut the `b`-part is: `conj(c_b) · ρ₁ᵇ_{pk} · ρ₂ᵇ_{qj} · conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{rl}) · conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sj})`.\n\nThe indices don't match: `a`-part has `(pi, qk, ri, sl)` while `b`-part has `(pk, qj, rl, sj)`. So it's NOT `|·|²`.\n\nHmm. So the direct expansion doesn't work for `G⁴` with the plaquette `g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹`.\n\nWait, but I know that `χ_λ(gh⁻¹)` IS PD on `G × G`. Can I use this iteratively?\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = χ_λ((g₁g₂)(g₃g₄)⁻¹)`... no, `(g₃g₄)⁻¹ = g₄⁻¹g₃⁻¹ ≠ g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹`.\n\nBut `χ_λ` is a class function: `χ_λ(AB) = χ_λ(BA)`. So `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = χ_λ(g₄⁻¹g₁g₂g₃⁻¹) = χ_λ((g₄⁻¹g₁)(g₂g₃⁻¹))`... still not `gh⁻¹`.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. Actually, `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = χ_λ(g₁g₂ · (g₄g₃)⁻¹)` because `(g₄g₃)⁻¹ = g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹`. Yes!\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = χ_λ((g₁g₂)(g₄g₃)⁻¹)`.\n\nNow, if I define `G' = G × G` (pairs) and `Φ: G' → G` by `Φ(g₁,g₂) = g₁g₂`, then `χ_λ(Φ(g₁,g₂) · Φ(g₄,g₃)⁻¹) = χ_λ((g₁g₂)(g₄g₃)⁻¹)`.\n\nBut `Φ` is NOT a group homomorphism from `G × G` to `G` (since `(g₁g₂)(h₁h₂) ≠ (g₁h₁)(g₂h₂)` in general for non-abelian `G`).\n\nSo I can't directly use the `χ_λ(gh⁻¹)` PD result on `G' = G × G`.\n\nOK, let me think about this more carefully. The issue is that the plaquette involves a product of 4 group elements, and we need PD on `G⁴`.\n\nActually, I think the correct approach is to use the Peter-Weyl theorem more carefully. The key fact is:\n\n**The function `(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) ↦ χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is PD on `G⁴`.**\n\nThis can be proven using the matrix coefficient expansion, but it requires a more careful grouping. Let me try again.\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ)`.\n\nLet me use the cyclic property: `Tr(ABCD) = Tr(DABC) = Tr(CDAB) = ...`.\n\n`Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ)`.\n\nNow, `Tr(ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ · ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄), ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ⟩_HS`... no, `Tr(AᴴB) = ⟨A, B⟩_HS`, so `Tr(ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ · M) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄), M⟩_HS` where `M = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ`.\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄), ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ⟩_HS = ∑_{i,j} conj(ρ_λ(g₄)_{ij}) (ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ)_{ij}`.\n\nNow, `(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ)_{ij} = ∑_{k,l} ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kl} conj(ρ_λ(g₃)_{jl})`.\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ∑_{i,j,k,l} conj(ρ_λ(g₄)_{ij}) ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kl} conj(ρ_λ(g₃)_{jl})`.\n\nFor the PD sum:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b)`\n\n`χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ∑_{i,j,k,l} conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)_{ij}) ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)_{kl} conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)_{jl})`\n\nExpanding:\n- `conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)_{ij}) = ∑_p ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{pi} conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{pj})`\n- `ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)_{ik} = ∑_q conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)_{qi}) ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{qk}`\n- `ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)_{kl} = ∑_r conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)_{rk}) ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{rl}`\n- `conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)_{jl}) = ∑_s ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{sj} conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{sl})`\n\nSo:\n`χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ∑_{i,j,k,l,p,q,r,s} [ρ₄ᵃ_{pi} conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{pj})] [conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{qi}) ρ₁ᵇ_{qk}] [conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{rk}) ρ₂ᵇ_{rl}] [ρ₃ᵃ_{sj} conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sl})]`\n\n`a`-dependent: `ρ₄ᵃ_{pi}, conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{qi}), conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{rk}), ρ₃ᵃ_{sj})`.\n`b`-dependent: `conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{pj}), ρ₁ᵇ_{qk}, ρ₂ᵇ_{rl}, conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sl})`.\n\nThe `a`-part (with `c_a`): `c_a · ρ₄ᵃ_{pi} · conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{qi}) · conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{rk}) · ρ₃ᵃ_{sj})`.\nThe conjugate of `a`-part (with `a→b`): `conj(c_b) · conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{pi}) · ρ₁ᵇ_{qi} · ρ₂ᵇ_{rk} · conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sj})`.\n\nThe `b`-part: `conj(c_b) · conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{pj}) · ρ₁ᵇ_{qk} · ρ₂ᵇ_{rl} · conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sl})`.\n\nAgain, the indices don't match. The `a`-part has free indices `(p,q,r,s)` with `i,j,k,l` summed, while the conjugate has `(p,q,r,s)` with `i,j,k,l` summed but in different positions.\n\nThis is frustrating. The issue is that the four matrix elements are \"chained\" through shared indices `(i,k,l,j)` and the chaining doesn't allow a clean factorization into `|·|²`.\n\nLet me try a completely different approach. Instead of expanding into individual matrix coefficients, let me use the Hilbert-Schmidt inner product directly.\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃), ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)⟩_HS` (using the cyclic property and the fact that `(ρ_λ(g₃)ρ_λ(g₄))ᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄)ᴴρ_λ(g₃)ᴴ`).\n\nWait, let me recheck. `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂) · (ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃))⁻¹)`.\n\nNow `(ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃))⁻¹ = ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹ = ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ = (ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃))ᴴ`.\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂) · (ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃))ᴴ) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃), ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)⟩_HS`.\n\nLet `A(g₁,g₂) = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` and `B(g₃,g₄) = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)` (note reversed order for B).\n\nThen `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨B(g₃,g₄), A(g₁,g₂)⟩_HS = ∑_{i,j} conj(B(g₃,g₄)_{ij}) A(g₁,g₂)_{ij}`.\n\nNow, `A(g₁,g₂)_{ij} = ∑_k ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj}` and `B(g₃,g₄)_{ij} = ∑_l ρ_λ(g₄)_{il} ρ_λ(g₃)_{lj}`.\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ∑_{i,j} A(g₁,g₂)_{ij} conj(B(g₃,g₄)_{ij}) = ∑_{i,j} f_{ij}(g₁,g₂) conj(f_{ij}(g₃,g₄))` where `f_{ij}(g₁,g₂) = A(g₁,g₂)_{ij} = ∑_k ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj}`.\n\nWait, but `B(g₃,g₄)_{ij} = ∑_l ρ_λ(g₄)_{il} ρ_λ(g₃)_{lj}`, which is NOT the same as `A(g₃,g₄)_{ij} = ∑_l ρ_λ(g₃)_{il} ρ_λ(g₄)_{lj}`. The order is reversed!\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ∑_{i,j} f_{ij}(g₁,g₂) conj(g_{ij}(g₃,g₄))` where `f_{ij}(g₁,g₂) = (ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂))_{ij}` and `g_{ij}(g₃,g₄) = (ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃))_{ij}`.\n\nNote that `g_{ij}(g₃,g₄) = f_{ij}(g₄,g₃)` (just swapping the arguments). So:\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ∑_{i,j} f_{ij}(g₁,g₂) conj(f_{ij}(g₄,g₃))`.\n\nNow, for the PD sum on `G⁴`:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b)`\n\nwhere `x_a = (g₁ᵃ, g₂ᵃ, g₃ᵃ, g₄ᵃ)`.\n\n`χ_λ(x_a⁻¹x_b) = ∑_{i,j} f_{ij}(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ) conj(f_{ij}(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ, g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ))`.\n\nNow, `f_{ij}(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ) = (ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ))_{ij} = ∑_k ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)_{kj}`.\n\nAnd `f_{ij}(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ, g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ) = (ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ))_{ij} = ∑_l ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)_{il} ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)_{lj}`.\n\nNow, `ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)`, so:\n`f_{ij}(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ) = ∑_k (ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ))_{ik} (ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ))_{kj}`\n`= ∑_k (∑_p conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)_{pi}) ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk}) (∑_q conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)_{qk}) ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj})`\n`= ∑_{k,p,q} conj(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)_{pi}) ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)_{pk} conj(ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)_{qk}) ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)_{qj}`.\n\nSimilarly:\n`f_{ij}(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ, g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ) = ∑_{l,r,s} conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{ri}) ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{rl} conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{sl}) ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{sj}`.\n\nSo:\n`conj(f_{ij}(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ, g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)) = ∑_{l,r,s} ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)_{ri} conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)_{rl}) ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)_{sl} conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)_{sj})`.\n\nThe PD sum:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) ∑_{i,j,k,l,p,q,r,s} [conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{pi}) ρ₁ᵇ_{pk} conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{qk}) ρ₂ᵇ_{qj}] [ρ₄ᵃ_{ri} conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{rl}) ρ₃ᵃ_{sl} conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sj})]`\n\nThe `a`-dependent factors: `conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{pi}), conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{qk}), ρ₄ᵃ_{ri}, ρ₃ᵃ_{sl})`.\nThe `b`-dependent factors: `ρ₁ᵇ_{pk}, ρ₂ᵇ_{qj}, conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{rl}), conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sj})`.\n\nThe `a`-part (with `c_a`): `c_a · conj(ρ₁ᵃ_{pi}) · conj(ρ₂ᵃ_{qk}) · ρ₄ᵃ_{ri} · ρ₃ᵃ_{sl})`.\nThe conjugate of `a`-part (with `a→b`): `conj(c_b) · ρ₁ᵇ_{pi} · ρ₂ᵇ_{qk} · conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{ri}) · conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sl})`.\n\nThe `b`-part: `conj(c_b) · ρ₁ᵇ_{pk} · ρ₂ᵇ_{qj} · conj(ρ₄ᵇ_{rl}) · conj(ρ₃ᵇ_{sj})`.\n\nThe indices in the `a`-part are `(p,i), (q,k), (r,i), (s,l)` — note `i` appears in both `p` and `r` terms, `k` in `q`, `l` in `s`.\nThe indices in the `b`-part are `(p,k), (q,j), (r,l), (s,j)` — note `j` appears in both `q` and `s` terms, `k` in `p`, `l` in `r`.\n\nSo the `a`-part has shared index `i` (between `p` and `r`), while the `b`-part has shared index `j` (between `q` and `s`). These are different summation indices, so the `a`-part and `b`-part have different structures, and it's NOT `|·|²`.\n\nHmm. So the direct matrix coefficient expansion doesn't give a clean `|·|²` for `G⁴` with the plaquette `g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹`.\n\nWait, but I know that `χ_λ(gh⁻¹)` IS PD on `G × G`. Can I use this iteratively?\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = χ_λ((g₁g₂)(g₄g₃)⁻¹)` (as I noted earlier).\n\nNow, define `h₁ = g₁g₂` and `h₂ = g₄g₃`. Then `χ_λ(h₁h₂⁻¹)` is PD on `G × G` (as a function of `(h₁, h₂)`).\n\nBut `h₁ = g₁g₂` is a function of `(g₁, g₂)` and `h₂ = g₄g₃` is a function of `(g₃, g₄)`. The map `(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) ↦ (h₁, h₂) = (g₁g₂, g₄g₃)` is NOT a group homomorphism from `G⁴` to `G × G` (because multiplication is not a homomorphism for non-abelian groups).\n\nSo I can't directly conclude that the pullback of a PD function is PD.\n\nHmm, but actually, the PD property is preserved under group homomorphisms (pullback by a homomorphism is PD), but NOT under arbitrary maps. The multiplication map `m: G × G → G` is NOT a homomorphism for non-abelian `G`, so the pullback of a PD function by `m` is NOT necessarily PD.\n\nThis is exactly the obstruction described in found_issues.md §3!\n\nSo how does the Osterwalder-Seiler proof actually work?\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. I think the key is that the character expansion `exp(c · Re Tr(g)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g)` combined with the specific structure of the transfer matrix kernel gives positivity, but NOT through the PD of the plaquette Boltzmann factor on `G⁴`.\n\nActually, let me re-read the Osterwalder-Seiler argument more carefully. The transfer matrix positivity comes from the fact that the kernel is a positive-definite function on the group of link variables. But the group of link variables is a PRODUCT group `G^L` (product over all links), and the kernel is a product of plaquette factors.\n\nFor a product group `G^L`, a function is PD if and only if... well, a product of PD functions on different factors is PD (by `PositiveDefinite.prod`). But each plaquette factor `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹))` is a function of 4 link variables, not 1.\n\nSo the question is: is `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹))` PD on `G⁴`?\n\nI've been trying to prove this and running into difficulties with the direct matrix coefficient approach. Let me think about whether it's actually true.\n\nActually, let me try a different approach. The character expansion gives:\n`exp(c · Re Tr(g)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g)` with `a_λ ≥ 0`.\n\nSo `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`.\n\nIf `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is PD on `G⁴` for each `λ`, then the sum with non-negative coefficients is PD.\n\nSo the question reduces to: is `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` PD on `G⁴`?\n\nLet me try to prove this using a different decomposition. Instead of expanding into individual matrix coefficients, let me use the fact that `χ_λ(gh⁻¹)` is PD on `G × G` and try to build up.\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = χ_λ((g₁g₂)(g₄g₃)⁻¹)`.\n\nNow, consider the map `φ: G⁴ → G × G` defined by `φ(g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄) = (g₁g₂, g₄g₃)`. This is NOT a group homomorphism.\n\nBut wait — maybe I should think about this differently. The PD property on `G⁴` is:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) F(x_a⁻¹x_b) ≥ 0` where `x_a = (g₁ᵃ, g₂ᵃ, g₃ᵃ, g₄ᵃ)` and `F(g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄) = χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`.\n\n`F(x_a⁻¹x_b) = χ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ · g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ · (g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ)⁻¹ · (g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)⁻¹) = χ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇg₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇg₃ᵇ⁻¹g₃ᵃg₄ᵇ⁻¹g₄ᵃ)`.\n\nThis is NOT `χ_λ((g₁ᵃg₂ᵃ)⁻¹(g₁ᵇg₂ᵇ) · ((g₄ᵃg₃ᵃ)⁻¹(g₄ᵇg₃ᵇ))⁻¹)` because `(g₁ᵃg₂ᵃ)⁻¹(g₁ᵇg₂ᵇ) = g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇg₂ᵇ ≠ g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇg₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ`.\n\nSo the group operation on `G⁴` (componentwise) doesn't interact well with the multiplication map.\n\nOK, I think I need to take a step back and think about what's actually true.\n\nLet me consider the simplest case: `G = SU(2)`, `λ` = fundamental representation, and check if `χ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is PD on `G⁴`.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully using the Hilbert-Schmidt approach.\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃), ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)⟩_HS`.\n\nLet `V = Mat_n(ℂ)` be the space of `n×n` matrices with the Hilbert-Schmidt inner product. Define:\n- `A: G² → V` by `A(g₁,g₂) = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)`\n- `B: G² → V` by `B(g₃,g₄) = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)`\n\nThen `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨B(g₃,g₄), A(g₁,g₂)⟩_V = ∑_{m} conj(B(g₃,g₄)_m) A(g₁,g₂)_m` where `m` indexes a basis of `V`.\n\nNow, `∑_{m} A(g₁,g₂)_m conj(B(g₃,g₄)_m)` is a sum of terms `f_m(g₁,g₂) conj(g_m(g₃,g₄))` where `f_m = A_m` and `g_m = B_m`.\n\nFor the PD sum on `G⁴`:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) ∑_m f_m(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ) conj(g_m(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ))`\n\nNow, `f_m(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ) = A_m(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)`. And `A(g₁,g₂) = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)`, so `A(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)`.\n\nSimilarly, `g_m(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ) = B_m(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)` and `B(g₃,g₄) = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)`, so `B(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)`.\n\nSo `conj(g_m(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)) = conj(B_m(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ))`.\n\nNow, `conj(B(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)) = conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵇ))`.\n\nUsing `conj(Mᴴ) = Mᵀ` (conjugate of conjugate-transpose is transpose) and `conj(MN) = conj(M)conj(N)`:\n`conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)) = conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ᴴ) conj(ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)) conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ᴴ) conj(ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ))`\n`= ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ᵀ ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)̄ ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ᵀ ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)̄`.\n\nHmm, this is getting very messy with transposes and conjugates. Let me try a different approach.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that `A(g₁,g₂) = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` is a matrix coefficient of a representation of `G × G`... no, it's not, because `ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` is not a representation of `G × G` (the representation would be `ρ_λ(g₁) ⊗ ρ_λ(g₂)`, which is different from `ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)`).\n\nWait, actually, `ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` is a matrix (an element of `Mat_n(ℂ)`), and it's a function of `(g₁, g₂)`. Is it a matrix coefficient of some representation of `G × G`?\n\nConsider the representation `π: G × G → U(V ⊗ V)` defined by `π(g₁, g₂) = ρ_λ(g₁) ⊗ ρ_λ(g₂)`. Then `π(g₁, g₂)(v ⊗ w) = ρ_λ(g₁)v ⊗ ρ_λ(g₂)w`.\n\nThe matrix coefficient `⟨π(g₁,g₂)(e_i ⊗ e_j), e_k ⊗ e_l⟩ = ⟨ρ_λ(g₁)e_i, e_k⟩ ⟨ρ_λ(g₂)e_j, e_l⟩ = ρ_λ(g₁)_{ki} ρ_λ(g₂)_{lj}`.\n\nSo `ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj}` (summed over `k`) is NOT a matrix coefficient of `π`. It's a contraction of matrix coefficients.\n\nHmm, actually, `∑_k ρ_λ(g₁)_{ik} ρ_λ(g₂)_{kj} = (ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂))_{ij}`. This is the `(i,j)` entry of the matrix product. It's a matrix coefficient of the representation `ρ_λ` evaluated at the product `g₁g₂`, but `g₁g₂` is not a group element of `G × G`.\n\nOK, I think I need to approach this problem differently. Let me think about what the Osterwalder-Seiler proof actually uses.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that the transfer matrix positivity doesn't require the plaquette Boltzmann factor to be PD on `G⁴`. Instead, it uses the character expansion to decompose the kernel into a sum of products of PD functions, where the decomposition comes from the Peter-Weyl theorem applied to each link variable separately.\n\nThe correct decomposition is:\n\n`exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`\n\nand then `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is decomposed using the Peter-Weyl theorem. The key fact is:\n\n`χ_λ(gh) = ∑_{μ,ν} N^λ_{μν} χ_μ(g) χ_ν(h)` — NO, this is the tensor product decomposition, which applies to `χ_μ(g)χ_ν(g)` (same argument), not `χ_λ(gh)` (product argument).\n\nActually, I think the correct fact is different. Let me think about what `χ_λ(gh)` expands to in the Peter-Weyl basis.\n\nThe Peter-Weyl theorem says that the matrix coefficients `√(dim V_λ) · ρ_λ(g)_{ij}` form an orthonormal basis of `L²(G)`. The characters `χ_λ = ∑_i ρ_λ(g)_{ii}` are sums of diagonal matrix coefficients.\n\nNow, `χ_λ(gh) = ∑_i ρ_λ(gh)_{ii} = ∑_{i,j} ρ_λ(g)_{ij} ρ_λ(h)_{ji}`.\n\nEach `ρ_λ(g)_{ij}` is a matrix coefficient (function of `g`), and `ρ_λ(h)_{ji}` is a matrix coefficient (function of `h`). So `χ_λ(gh) = ∑_{i,j} u^λ_{ij}(g) u^λ_{ji}(h)` (up to normalization).\n\nNow, `u^λ_{ij}(g) u^λ_{ji}(h)` is a product of a function of `g` and a function of `h`. As a function on `G × G`, it's a matrix coefficient of the representation `λ ⊗ λ` of `G × G` (specifically, the coefficient `⟨π(g,h)(e_j ⊗ e_i), e_i ⊗ e_j⟩` where `π(g,h) = ρ_λ(g) ⊗ ρ_λ(h)`).\n\nWait, `⟨π(g,h)(e_j ⊗ e_i), e_i ⊗ e_j⟩ = ⟨ρ_λ(g)e_j ⊗ ρ_λ(h)e_i, e_i ⊗ e_j⟩ = ⟨ρ_λ(g)e_j, e_i⟩ ⟨ρ_λ(h)e_i, e_j⟩ = ρ_λ(g)_{ij} ρ_λ(h)_{ji}`. Yes!\n\nSo `χ_λ(gh) = ∑_{i,j} ⟨π(g,h)(e_j ⊗ e_i), e_i ⊗ e_j⟩` where `π = ρ_λ ⊗ ρ_λ` is a representation of `G × G`.\n\nNow, `⟨π(g,h)(e_j ⊗ e_i), e_i ⊗ e_j⟩` is a matrix coefficient of `π`. But it's NOT a diagonal matrix coefficient (unless `i = j`), so it's NOT PD in general.\n\nHowever, `χ_λ(gh) = ∑_{i,j} ⟨π(g,h)(e_j ⊗ e_i), e_i ⊗ e_j⟩`. We can rewrite this as:\n\n`χ_λ(gh) = ⟨π(g,h) Ω, Ω⟩` where `Ω = ∑_i e_i ⊗ e_i` (the \"maximally entangled\" state).\n\nWait, `⟨π(g,h) Ω, Ω⟩ = ⟨∑_i ρ_λ(g)e_i ⊗ ρ_λ(h)e_i, ∑_j e_j ⊗ e_j⟩ = ∑_{i,j} ⟨ρ_λ(g)e_i, e_j⟩ ⟨ρ_λ(h)e_i, e_j⟩ = ∑_{i,j} ρ_λ(g)_{ji} ρ_λ(h)_{ji}`.\n\nHmm, that's `∑_{i,j} ρ_λ(g)_{ji} ρ_λ(h)_{ji}`, which is NOT `χ_λ(gh) = ∑_{i,j} ρ_λ(g)_{ij} ρ_λ(h)_{ji}`.\n\nLet me try `Ω = ∑_i e_i ⊗ e_i` and a different pairing. Actually, `χ_λ(gh) = Tr(ρ_λ(g)ρ_λ(h)) = ∑_i (ρ_λ(g)ρ_λ(h))_{ii} = ∑_{i,j} ρ_λ(g)_{ij} ρ_λ(h)_{ji}`.\n\nConsider the bilinear form `B(v, w) = ∑_{i,j} v_{ij} w_{ji} = Tr(vw)` on `Mat_n(ℂ)`. Then `χ_λ(gh) = B(ρ_λ(g), ρ_λ(h))`.\n\nNow, `B(v, w) = Tr(vw) = ∑_i (vw)_{ii} = ∑_{i,j} v_{ij} w_{ji}`. This is NOT the Hilbert-Schmidt inner product `⟨v, w⟩_HS = Tr(wᴴv) = ∑_{i,j} conj(w_{ij}) v_{ij}`.\n\nBut `Tr(vw) = Tr(wv) = ∑_{i,j} w_{ij} v_{ji} = ∑_{i,j} v_{ji} w_{ij}`. And `⟨w̄, v⟩_HS = ∑_{i,j} conj(w̄_{ij}) v_{ij} = ∑_{i,j} w_{ij} v_{ij}`... no, `conj(w̄_{ij}) = w_{ij}`, so `⟨w̄, v⟩_HS = ∑_{i,j} w_{ij} v_{ij}`. That's the Frobenius inner product without conjugation, which is `Tr(vwᵀ)`... no.\n\nOK this is getting confusing. Let me just think about the PD property directly.\n\nFor `χ_λ(gh⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g)ρ_λ(h)⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g)ρ_λ(h)ᴴ) = ⟨ρ_λ(h), ρ_λ(g)⟩_HS`:\n\nThe PD sum on `G × G`:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) χ_λ(g_a⁻¹g_b, h_a⁻¹h_b⁻¹)`... \n\nwait, `F(g,h) = χ_λ(gh⁻¹)`, so `F((g_a,h_a)⁻¹(g_b,h_b)) = F(g_a⁻¹g_b, h_a⁻¹h_b) = χ_λ((g_a⁻¹g_b)(h_a⁻¹h_b)⁻¹) = χ_λ(g_a⁻¹g_b h_b⁻¹h_a)`.\n\n`= Tr(ρ_λ(g_a⁻¹g_b)ρ_λ(h_b⁻¹h_a)) = Tr(ρ_λ(g_a)ᴴρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴρ_λ(h_a))`.\n\nUsing cyclic property: `= Tr(ρ_λ(h_a)ρ_λ(g_a)ᴴρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ) = Tr((ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ)ᴴ ρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ) = ⟨ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ, ρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ⟩_HS`.\n\nWait, `Tr(AᴴB) = ⟨A, B⟩_HS`. So `Tr((ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ)ᴴ · ρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ) = ⟨ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ, ρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ⟩_HS`.\n\nBut `(ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ)ᴴ = ρ_λ(h_a)ρ_λ(g_a)ᴴ`. So `Tr(ρ_λ(h_a)ρ_λ(g_a)ᴴ · ρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ) = ⟨ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ, ρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ⟩_HS`... \n\nHmm, `Tr(AᴴB) = ⟨A, B⟩_HS`. So if I want `⟨M_a, M_b⟩_HS = Tr(M_aᴴ M_b)`, I need `M_aᴴ M_b = ρ_λ(h_a)ρ_λ(g_a)ᴴ · ρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ`, so `M_aᴴ = ρ_λ(h_a)ρ_λ(g_a)ᴴ`, meaning `M_a = ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ`.\n\nSo `F((g_a,h_a)⁻¹(g_b,h_b)) = ⟨ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ, ρ_λ(g_b)ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ⟩_HS = ∑_m conj(M_a_m) M_b_m` where `M_a = ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ` and `m` indexes a basis.\n\nThe PD sum: `∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) ∑_m conj(M_a_m) M_b_m = ∑_m (∑_b conj(c_b) M_b_m) (∑_a c_a conj(M_a_m)) = ∑_m |∑_a c_a conj(M_a_m)|²`... \n\nwait, `∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) conj(M_a_m) M_b_m = (∑_a c_a conj(M_a_m)) (∑_b conj(c_b) M_b_m) = (∑_a c_a conj(M_a_m)) conj(∑_b c_b conj(M_b_m)) = |∑_a c_a conj(M_a_m)|²`.\n\nYes! So the PD sum is `∑_m |∑_a c_a conj(M_a_m)|² ≥ 0`. This proves `χ_λ(gh⁻¹)` is PD on `G × G`.\n\nNow, for `G⁴` with `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`:\n\n`F(g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄) = χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ)`.\n\n`F(x_a⁻¹x_b) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ))`.\n\nUsing cyclic property: `= Tr(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴ)`.\n\nHmm, let me try to write this as `Tr(M_aᴴ M_b)` for some `M_a, M_b`.\n\n`Tr(M_aᴴ M_b)` where `M_a` depends on `x_a = (g₁ᵃ, g₂ᵃ, g₃ᵃ, g₄ᵃ)` and `M_b` depends on `x_b`.\n\nFrom the cyclic rearrangement: `Tr(ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴ · ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴ)`.\n\nFor this to be `Tr(M_aᴴ M_b)`, I need `M_aᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴ` and `M_b = ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴ`. But `M_b` depends on both `a` and `b` (through `g₂ᵃ` and `g₃ᵃ`), so this doesn't work.\n\nLet me try a different cyclic rearrangement. \n\n`Tr(ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ))`\n\nLet me group as: `Tr([ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)] · [ρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)])`.\n\nThe first bracket depends on `(g₁ᵃ, g₂ᵃ, g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵇ)` and the second on `(g₃ᵃ, g₄ᵃ, g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵇ)`. But each bracket depends on BOTH `a` and `b`, so we can't separate them.\n\nHmm. Let me try yet another grouping. What if I use the Hilbert-Schmidt inner product on a larger space?\n\nDefine `M_a = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ) ⊗ ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)` (tensor product, living in `V ⊗ V` where `V = Mat_n(ℂ)`). Then... no, this doesn't directly help.\n\nActually, let me think about this problem from a higher level. The issue is that `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` involves a product of 4 group elements, and the PD property on `G⁴` requires separating the `a` and `b` indices.\n\nFor `G × G` (2 factors), the key was that `χ_λ(gh⁻¹) = ⟨ρ_λ(g)ρ_λ(h)ᴴ, I⟩_HS`... no, `χ_λ(gh⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g)ρ_λ(h)ᴴ) = ⟨ρ_λ(h), ρ_λ(g)⟩_HS`, and the PD sum worked because `M_a = ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ` depends on `x_a = (g_a, h_a)` only.\n\nFor `G⁴` (4 factors), `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ)`. The issue is that this is `Tr(A · B)` where `A = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` and `B = ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ`, and `Tr(AB) ≠ ⟨A, B⟩_HS` in general (it's `⟨Bᴴ, A⟩_HS` or something).\n\nActually, `Tr(AB) = ∑_{i,j} A_{ij} B_{ji}`. And `⟨C, A⟩_HS = Tr(CᴴA) = ∑_{i,j} conj(C_{ij}) A_{ij}`. So `Tr(AB) = ⟨C, A⟩_HS` requires `conj(C_{ij}) = B_{ji}`, i.e., `C = Bᴴ`. So `Tr(AB) = ⟨Bᴴ, A⟩_HS`.\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(AB) = ⟨Bᴴ, A⟩_HS` where `A = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` and `B = ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ = (ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃))ᴴ`.\n\nSo `Bᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)` and `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃), ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)⟩_HS`.\n\nNow, `A = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` depends on `(g₁, g₂)` and `Bᴴ = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)` depends on `(g₃, g₄)`.\n\nSo `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨C(g₃,g₄), A(g₁,g₂)⟩_HS` where `A(g₁,g₂) = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` and `C(g₃,g₄) = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)`.\n\nNow, `⟨C, A⟩_HS = ∑_m conj(C_m) A_m` where `m` indexes a basis of `Mat_n(ℂ)`.\n\nThe PD sum on `G⁴`:\n`∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) ⟨C(x_a⁻¹x_b|₃₄), A(x_a⁻¹x_b|₁₂)⟩_HS`\n\nwhere `x_a⁻¹x_b|₁₂ = (g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ)` and `x_a⁻¹x_b|₃₄ = (g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)`.\n\n`A(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ, g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ⁻¹g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ⁻¹g₂ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)`.\n\n`C(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ) = ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵇ)`.\n\nSo `conj(C(g₃ᵃ⁻¹g₃ᵇ, g₄ᵃ⁻¹g₄ᵇ)_m) = conj((ρ_λ(g₄ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₄ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₃ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₃ᵇ))_m)`.\n\nThe PD sum: `∑_{a,b} c_a conj(c_b) ∑_m conj(C_ab_m) A_ab_m` where `A_ab = A(x_a⁻¹x_b|₁₂)` and `C_ab = C(x_a⁻¹x_b|₃₄)`.\n\nNow, `A_ab = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)`. This depends on both `a` and `b`.\n\nThe issue is that `A_ab` is NOT of the form `P_aᴴ P_b` for some `P_a` depending only on `a`. If it were, then `∑_m conj(C_ab_m) A_ab_m = ⟨C_ab, A_ab⟩_HS = ⟨Q_a, P_a⟩ · ...` and we could separate.\n\n`A_ab = ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)`. Can this be written as `P_aᴴ P_b`?\n\n`P_aᴴ P_b` would require `P_a` to depend only on `a` and `P_b` only on `b`. But `A_ab` has `ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴ` (depends on `a`), `ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)` (depends on `b`), `ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴ` (depends on `a`), `ρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)` (depends on `b`). The `a` and `b` factors are interleaved: `a, b, a, b`. For `P_aᴴ P_b`, we'd need all `a` factors on the left and all `b` factors on the right: `a, a, b, b`.\n\nBut `ρ_λ(g₁ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)ρ_λ(g₂ᵃ)ᴴρ_λ(g₂ᵇ)` has the `b` factor `ρ_λ(g₁ᵇ)` sandwiched between `a` factors. We can't commute it out (non-commutative!).\n\nSo the direct Hilbert-Schmidt approach doesn't work for `G⁴` because the `a` and `b` factors are interleaved.\n\nThis is the fundamental obstruction. For `G × G`, the character `χ_λ(gh⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g)ρ_λ(h)ᴴ)` has the form `Tr(A_a · B_b)` where `A_a = ρ_λ(g_a)` and `B_b = ρ_λ(h_b)ᴴ`, and the cyclic property allows us to write it as `⟨M_a, M_b⟩_HS` with `M_a = ρ_λ(g_a)ρ_λ(h_a)ᴴ`.\n\nFor `G⁴`, the character `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)ᴴρ_λ(g₄)ᴴ)` has the form `Tr(A₁_a A₂_a B₁_b B₂_b)`... no, it's `Tr(A₁_a A₂_a B₃_aᴴ B₄_aᴴ)` for a single point, but for the PD sum it becomes `Tr(A₁_aᴴ A₁_b A₂_aᴴ A₂_b B₃_bᴴ B₃_a B₄_bᴴ B₄_a)` which has interleaved `a` and `b` factors.\n\nSo the question is: is `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` actually PD on `G⁴`?\n\nLet me try a small example. Take `G = SU(2)`, `λ` = fundamental, `χ(g) = Tr(g)`. Take 2 points in `G⁴`:\n- `x_1 = (I, I, I, I)` (all identity)\n- `x_2 = (iσ₁, iσ₂, iσ₃, I)` (Pauli matrices for first 3, identity for 4th)\n\n`c_1 = 1, c_2 = 1`.\n\n`F(x_1⁻¹x_1) = χ(I·I·I·I) = Tr(I) = 2`.\n`F(x_1⁻¹x_2) = χ(iσ₁ · iσ₂ · (iσ₃)⁻¹ · I⁻¹) = χ(iσ₁ · iσ₂ · (-iσ₃) · I) = χ(i²σ₁σ₂ · (-iσ₃)) = χ(-σ₃ · (-iσ₃)) = χ(iσ₃²) = χ(i·(-I)) = χ(-iI) = Tr(-iI) = -2i`.\n\nWait, let me recompute. `iσ₁ · iσ₂ = i²σ₁σ₂ = -σ₁σ₂ = -iσ₃` (since `σ₁σ₂ = iσ₃`). And `(iσ₃)⁻¹ = (-iσ₃)⁻¹`... wait, `(iσ₃)⁻¹ = (iσ₃)⁻¹`. Since `(iσ₃)² = i²σ₃² = -I`, we have `(iσ₃)⁻¹ = -iσ₃`... no, `(iσ₃)⁻¹ = (iσ₃)ᴴ / |iσ₃|²`. Since `iσ₃` is unitary, `(iσ₃)⁻¹ = (iσ₃)ᴴ = -iσ₃ᴴ = -iσ₃` (since `σ₃` is Hermitian). Wait, `(iσ₃)ᴴ = conj(i)σ₃ᴴ = -iσ₃`. So `(iσ₃)⁻¹ = -iσ₃`.\n\nSo `F(x_1⁻¹x_2) = χ(iσ₁ · iσ₂ · (-iσ₃) · I) = χ((-iσ₃)(-iσ₃)) = χ((-i)²σ₃²) = χ(-1·(-I)) = χ(I) = 2`.\n\nHmm wait, let me recompute. `iσ₁ · iσ₂ = i²σ₁σ₂ = -σ₁σ₂`. And `σ₁σ₂ = iσ₃`. So `iσ₁ · iσ₂ = -iσ₃`. Then `(-iσ₃) · I = -iσ₃`. So `iσ₁ · iσ₂ · (-iσ₃) · I = (-iσ₃)(-iσ₃) = (-i)²σ₃² = (-i)²(-I) = (-1)(-I) = I`. Wait, `(-i)² = -1` and `σ₃² = I`, so `(-iσ₃)² = (-i)²σ₃² = (-1)(I) = -I`.\n\nSo `F(x_1⁻¹x_2) = χ(-I) = Tr(-I) = -2`.\n\n`F(x_2⁻¹x_1) = conj(F(x_1⁻¹x_2))` (by PD property `F(g⁻¹) = conj(F(g))`... but we're testing if F is PD, so we can't assume this). Let me compute directly.\n\n`x_2⁻¹x_1 = ((iσ₁)⁻¹, (iσ₂)⁻¹, (iσ₃)⁻¹, I⁻¹) = (-iσ₁, -iσ₂, -iσ₃, I)`.\n\n`F(x_2⁻¹x_1) = χ((-iσ₁)(-iσ₂)(-iσ₃)⁻¹ I) = χ((-iσ₁)(-iσ₂)(iσ₃)I)`.\n\n`(-iσ₁)(-iσ₂) = (-i)²σ₁σ₂ = -iσ₃`. So `(-iσ₃)(iσ₃) = (-i)(i)σ₃² = (-i²)(I) = (1)(I) = I`.\n\nWait, `(-iσ₃)(iσ₃) = (-i)(i)σ₃σ₃ = (-i²)σ₃² = (1)(I) = I`. So `F(x_2⁻¹x_1) = χ(I) = 2`.\n\nHmm, but `F(x_1⁻¹x_2) = -2` and `F(x_2⁻¹x_1) = 2`. For PD, we need `F(g⁻¹) = conj(F(g))`, so `F(x_2⁻¹x_1) = conj(F(x_1⁻¹x_2)) = conj(-2) = -2`. But I got `F(x_2⁻¹x_1) = 2 ≠ -2`. So either F is NOT PD, or I made a computation error.\n\nLet me recheck. `F(g) = χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` (for fundamental rep of SU(2)).\n\n`F(x_1⁻¹x_2)`: `x_1⁻¹x_2 = (I⁻¹·iσ₁, I⁻¹·iσ₂, I⁻¹·iσ₃, I⁻¹·I) = (iσ₁, iσ₂, iσ₃, I)`.\n\n`F(x_1⁻¹x_2) = Tr(iσ₁ · iσ₂ · (iσ₃)⁻¹ · I⁻¹) = Tr((-iσ₃)(-iσ₃)) = Tr(-I) = -2`.\n\n`F(x_2⁻¹x_1)`: `x_2⁻¹x_1 = ((iσ₁)⁻¹·I, (iσ₂)⁻¹·I, (iσ₃)⁻¹·I, I⁻¹·I) = (-iσ₁, -iσ₂, -iσ₃, I)`.\n\n`F(x_2⁻¹x_1) = Tr((-iσ₁)(-iσ₂)(-iσ₃)⁻¹ I⁻¹) = Tr((-iσ₁)(-iσ₂)(iσ₃))`.\n\n`(-iσ₁)(-iσ₂) = (-i)²σ₁σ₂ = (-1)(iσ₃) = -iσ₃`.\n\n`(-iσ₃)(iσ₃) = (-i)(i)σ₃² = (-i²)(I) = (1)(I) = I`.\n\nSo `F(x_2⁻¹x_1) = Tr(I) = 2`.\n\nBut `conj(F(x_1⁻¹x_2)) = conj(-2) = -2 ≠ 2 = F(x_2⁻¹x_1)`.\n\nSo `F` does NOT satisfy `F(g⁻¹) = conj(F(g))`, which means `F` is NOT PD!\n\nWait, but this contradicts the claim that the plaquette Boltzmann factor is PD. Let me double-check.\n\nActually, `F(g) = Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is a complex-valued function. For it to be PD, it must satisfy `F(g⁻¹) = conj(F(g))` (this is a necessary condition for PD, as proven in `PositiveDefinite.conj_inv`).\n\n`F(x_1⁻¹x_2) = -2` (real), `F(x_2⁻¹x_1) = 2` (real). `conj(F(x_1⁻¹x_2)) = -2 ≠ 2 = F(x_2⁻¹x_1)`.\n\nSo `Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is NOT PD on `SU(2)⁴`!\n\nThis means the character `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is NOT PD on `G⁴` for the fundamental representation of `SU(2)`.\n\nSo the approach of decomposing `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹))` into characters and using PD of each character on `G⁴` does NOT work!\n\nThis is a fundamental issue. Let me reconsider the Osterwalder-Seiler proof.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that I'm looking at `Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` (the character of the fundamental representation), but the character expansion of `exp(c · Re Tr(g))` involves ALL irreducible representations, not just the fundamental. And the COMBINATION `∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` might be PD even though individual `χ_λ` are not.\n\nBut that seems unlikely — the character expansion is a sum with non-negative coefficients, and if individual terms are not PD, the sum might not be either.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The function `exp(c · Re Tr(g))` is PD on `G` (proven in `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite`). But `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹))` is the pullback of this PD function by the map `(g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄) ↦ g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹`. This map is NOT a group homomorphism, so the pullback is NOT necessarily PD.\n\nSo `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹))` is NOT PD on `G⁴` in general!\n\nThis means the entire approach of proving the plaquette Boltzmann factor is PD on `G⁴` is WRONG. The Osterwalder-Seiler proof must use a different argument.\n\nLet me re-read the Osterwalder-Seiler paper's approach more carefully. Let me search for it.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that the transfer matrix positivity does NOT require the plaquette Boltzmann factor to be PD on `G⁴`. Instead, it uses the character expansion to decompose the transfer matrix kernel into a sum of POSITIVE OPERATORS, where each operator is a tensor product of operators acting on individual link variables.\n\nThe correct argument is:\n\n1. The transfer matrix `T` has a kernel that is a product of plaquette Boltzmann factors.\n2. Each plaquette Boltzmann factor `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹))` can be expanded using the character expansion: `exp(c · Re Tr(g)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g)`.\n3. So `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`.\n4. Now, `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)ρ_λ(g₃)⁻¹ρ_λ(g₄)⁻¹)`. This can be viewed as a KERNEL of an operator on `L²(G²)` (acting on the `(g₁, g₂)` variables, with `(g₃, g₄)` as the \"output\" variables).\n\nActually, I think the correct way to think about this is:\n\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃), ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)⟩_HS`.\n\nThis is the kernel of a rank-1 operator on `L²(G²)` (or rather, on the space of `V`-valued functions on `G²`): the operator that maps `ψ(g₃, g₄) ↦ ⟨ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃), ψ⟩ · ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)`... no, that's not quite right.\n\nActually, `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ⟨C(g₃,g₄), A(g₁,g₂)⟩_HS` where `A(g₁,g₂) = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` and `C(g₃,g₄) = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)`.\n\nThis is a rank-1 kernel: `K((g₁,g₂), (g₃,g₄)) = ⟨C(g₃,g₄), A(g₁,g₂)⟩`. The corresponding operator is `Tψ(g₁,g₂) = A(g₁,g₂) ∫ ⟨C(g₃,g₄), ψ(g₃,g₄)⟩ dμ(g₃,g₄)`, which is `|A⟩⟨C|`, a rank-1 operator. This is a POSITIVE operator only if `A = C` (up to a positive scalar), which is NOT the case here (`A(g₁,g₂) = ρ_λ(g₁)ρ_λ(g₂)` while `C(g₃,g₄) = ρ_λ(g₄)ρ_λ(g₃)`, different order).\n\nHmm, but `A(g,g') = ρ_λ(g)ρ_λ(g')` and `C(g,g') = ρ_λ(g')ρ_λ(g)`. These are NOT the same (non-commutative).\n\nSo the rank-1 operator `|A⟩⟨C|` is NOT positive in general.\n\nBut wait, the character expansion gives `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = ∑_λ a_λ ⟨C_λ, A_λ⟩`. This is a sum of rank-1 operators `|A_λ⟩⟨C_λ|` with non-negative coefficients `a_λ`. A sum of rank-1 operators is positive only if each rank-1 operator is positive, which requires `A_λ = C_λ` (up to positive scalar).\n\nBut `A_λ ≠ C_λ` in general! So this approach also doesn't work.\n\nI'm clearly missing something about the Osterwalder-Seiler proof. Let me search for the actual paper or a reference that explains the proof.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that I'm conflating the kernel of the transfer matrix with the plaquette Boltzmann factor. The transfer matrix kernel involves a PRODUCT of plaquette factors, and the specific structure of the OS decomposition (positive/negative/interface split) matters.\n\nLet me re-read the gap_analysis.md more carefully to understand the intended proof structure.\n\nActually, let me search for the Osterwalder-Seiler paper to understand the actual proof.","turn":19,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":34,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\gap_analysis.md\n-- 293 lines, 14276 bytes --\n141 | | Characters of irreps | Partial (finite groups) | `RepresentationTheory/Character` |\n142 | | Orthogonality of characters | Partial (finite groups) | |\n143 | | Positive-definite functions on groups | Not available | |\n144 | | Bochner theorem for compact groups | Not available | |\n145 | \n146 | ### 2. Transfer Matrix Framework\n147 | \n148 | **Nothing in Mathlib.** The following need to be built:\n149 | \n150 | | Component | Description |\n151 | |-----------|-------------|\n152 | | $L^2$ space on $\\text{SU}(N)^n$ | Built on existing measure theory |\n153 | | Integral operators on $L^2$ | As kernel operators |\n154 | | Positivity of operators | Spectral theory of bounded operators |\n155 | | Product measures and Fubini | Available in Mathlib |\n156 | \n157 | ### 3. Character Expansion of Wilson Action\n158 | \n159 | | Component | Description |\n160 | |-----------|-------------|\n161 | | Expansion of $\\exp(c \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g))$ in characters | Requires representation theory |\n162 | | Positivity of expansion coefficients | Known result (Osterwalder-Seiler) |\n163 | | Factorization over plaquettes | Already done algebraically |\n164 | \n165 | ## Alternative Approaches\n166 | \n167 | ### A. Product of PD on different factors (✅ PROVEN)\n168 | \n169 | The lemma `PositiveDefinite.prod` (in `PositiveDefinite.lean`) proves that if\n170 | `φ : G → ℂ` is positive-definite and `ψ : H → ℂ` is positive-definite, then\n171 | `(g, h) ↦ φ(g) * ψ(h)` is positive-definite on `G × H`.\n172 | \n173 | **Proof**: For any finset `s ⊂ G × H` and coefficients `c`, define matrices\n174 | `A_{ij} = φ(g_i⁻¹ g_j)` and `B_{ij} = ψ(h_i⁻¹ h_j)`. Both are PSD by the\n175 | grouping argument (`PositiveDefinite.sum_nonneg_of_map`), which handles\n176 | non-injective maps `f : α → G` by regrouping the quadratic form by fibers.\n177 | By the Schur product theorem (`Matrix.PosSemidef.hadamard`), `A ⊙ B` is PSD,\n178 | and the quadratic form of `A ⊙ B` equals the PD sum for the product function.\n179 | \n180 | This is one of the three ingredients needed for the character expansion approach\n181 | (see `docs/found_issues.md` §3). The remaining ingredients are:\n182 | 1. ✅ Product of PD on different factors — **PROVEN** (`PositiveDefinite.prod`)\n183 | 2. ❌ Peter-Weyl theorem: `exp(c·Re Tr(g)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g)` with `a_λ ≥ 0`\n184 | 3. ❌ Clebsch-Gordan decomposition: `χ_λ(gh) = ∑_{μ,ν} N^λ_{μν} χ_μ(g) χ_ν(h)`\n185 | \n186 | ### B. Heat Kernel Approach\n187 | \n188 | Instead of the character expansion, one could use the heat kernel on $\\text{SU}(N)$:\n189 | \n190 | $$\\exp\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{N} \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g)\\right) = \\int_{\\mathfrak{su}(N)} e^{\\langle X, \\log g \\rangle} \\, d\\mu_\\beta(X)$$\n191 | \n192 | where $\\mu_\\beta$ is a Gaussian measure on the Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{su}(N)$.\n193 | This represents the Boltzmann factor as the Fourier transform of a positive measure,\n194 | which is automatically positive-definite.\n195 | \n196 | **Status in Mathlib**: \n197 | - Gaussian measures: Available (`Mathlib/MeasureTheory/`)\n198 | - Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{su}(N)$: Defined as `LieAlgebraSU` in `SpecialUnitary.lean`\n199 | - Matrix exponential: Not available\n200 | - Fourier transform on Lie groups: Not available\n201 | \n202 | ### B. Reflection Positivity per Plaquette\n203 | \n204 | One could attempt to prove that each individual plaquette contribution is\n205 | reflection-positive, and then use the fact that the product of reflection-positive\n206 | functions is reflection-positive. This would reduce the problem to proving\n207 | reflection positivity for a single plaquette.\n208 | \n209 | **Status**: This is essentially equivalent to the character expansion approach,\n210 | since a single plaquette is the fundamental building block.\n211 | \n212 | ### C. Finite Lattice Spectral Argument\n213 | \n214 | For a strictly finite lattice, the transfer matrix is a finite-rank operator\n215 | (acting on a finite-dimensional space if we discretize the group... but SU(N) is\n216 | continuous, so still infinite-dimensional).\n217 | \n218 | ### Current Status\n219 | \n220 | The corrected transfer matrix framework has been implemented in `TransferMatrix.lean`:\n221 | \n222 | - **`reflectToPosInterface`**: The map θ⁻⁰ from negative+interface to positive+interface.\n223 | - **`g_posInterface`**: The restricted function `g(u) = f(u)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(u)/2)`.\n224 | - **`transferMatrixCorrect`**: The correct transfer matrix with reflection kernel.\n225 | - **`integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`**: ✅ PROVEN (0 sorries). The key identity.\n226 | - **`transferMatrixCorrect_positive`**: ❌ BLOCKED — requires Peter-Weyl theorem.\n227 |   See `docs/found_issues.md` §3 for the precise obstruction (counterexample showing\n228 |   `Tr(gh)` is not PD on `SU(N) × SU(N)`).\n229 | - **`integral_G_thetaG_nonneg'`**: Depends on the above.\n230 | \n231 | ### Remaining Steps:\n232 | \n233 | ### Short-term (measure-theoretic bookkeeping, no representation theory):\n234 | \n235 | 1. **Prove `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`**: ✅ DONE. The identity\n236 |    ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g(u)·(T g)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n237 |    is fully proven using `measure_factorization'`, the action reflection lemmas, Haar measure\n238 |    invariance under reflection, and the definition of `transferMatrixCorrect`.\n239 |    This is purely measure-theoretic — no representation theory is needed.\n240 | \n\n... 53 more line(s) below (use offset=241 to continue reading)","timestamp":1784915210,"token_count":1713,"tool_call_id":"61006f48-6a36-40b0-abe0-c3a411b579c2","full_token_estimate":1267,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"docs/gap_analysis.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":293,"byte_count":14276,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":19,"is_prune_marker":fal