{"id":37,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\gap_analysis.md\n-- 293 lines, 14276 bytes --\n241 | ### Medium-term (requires representation theory — BLOCKED):\n242 | \n243 | 2. **Prove `transferMatrixCorrect_positive`**: ❌ BLOCKED. The transfer matrix T is positive\n244 |    iff its kernel is a positive-definite function on the link-variable group. The kernel is a\n245 |    product of plaquette factors exp(c·Re Tr(U_∂p)), and each factor must be PD on SU(N)^4.\n246 | \n247 |    **Obstruction**: The function (g,h) ↦ Tr(gh) is NOT positive-definite on SU(N) × SU(N)\n248 |    for N ≥ 2 (counterexample with Pauli matrices, see `docs/found_issues.md` §3). The\n249 |    multiplication map SU(N)^k → SU(N) is NOT a group homomorphism for non-abelian groups,\n250 |    so composing a PD function with it does NOT preserve PD-ness.\n251 | \n252 |    **What's needed**: The character expansion (Peter-Weyl theorem) decomposes\n253 |    exp(c·Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃g₄)) into a sum of products χ_μ(g₁)·χ_ν(g₂)·χ_ρ(g₃)·χ_σ(g₄)\n254 |    with non-negative coefficients, where each factor is a PD function on SU(N). This\n255 |    requires:\n256 |    - Peter-Weyl theorem for compact Lie groups (NOT in Mathlib)\n257 |    - Clebsch-Gordan / Littlewood-Richardson decomposition (NOT in Mathlib)\n258 |    - Character expansion of exp(c·Re Tr(g)) with non-negative coefficients (NOT in Mathlib)\n259 | \n260 | 3. **Prove `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite`** (`PositiveDefinite.lean`): ✅ DONE.\n261 |    exp(c Re Tr(g)) is positive-definite on SU(N) for c ≥ 0. This is the core analytic fact\n262 |    needed for (2), but it is NOT SUFFICIENT by itself — the composition with the plaquette\n263 |    product map does not preserve PD-ness for non-abelian groups.\n264 | \n265 | ### Long-term (upstream to Mathlib):\n266 | \n267 | 1. **Peter-Weyl theorem** for compact Lie groups\n268 | 2. **Character theory** for compact (not just finite) groups\n269 | 3. **Positive-definite functions** and Bochner's theorem for compact groups\n270 | \n271 | 1. K. Osterwalder, E. Seiler, \"Gauge Field Theories on a Lattice\"\n272 |    (Ann. Phys. 110, 1978, pp 440–471), §3.\n273 |    - The original proof of reflection positivity for lattice gauge theories.\n274 |    - Uses the character expansion and Peter-Weyl theorem.\n275 | \n276 | 2. M. Lüscher, \"General Proof of Osterwalder-Schrader Positivity for the\n277 |    Wilson Action\" (Commun. Math. Phys. 113, 1987, pp 311–325).\n278 |    - Alternative proof using explicit transfer matrix construction.\n279 |    - Does not require fermions.\n280 | \n281 | 3. P. Menotti, A. Pelissetto, \"General Proof of Osterwalder-Schrader Positivity\n282 |    for the Wilson Action\" (Commun. Math. Phys. 113, 1987, pp 369–373).\n283 |    - Extension to reflections about planes containing sites.\n284 | \n285 | 4. J. Glimm, A. Jaffe, \"Quantum Physics\" (2nd ed.), §6.1.\n286 |    - Textbook treatment of reflection positivity.\n287 | \n288 | 5. M. Salmhofer, \"Construction of a Higgs field\" (thesis, 1990).\n289 |    - Detailed treatment of the transfer matrix for lattice gauge theories.\n290 | \n291 | 6. T. Bröcker, T. tom Dieck, \"Representations of Compact Lie Groups\"\n292 |    (Springer, 1985).\n293 |    - Standard reference for the Peter-Weyl theorem.\n","timestamp":1784915215,"token_count":999,"tool_call_id":"d0bf1673-1261-4bd9-9d71-737eefb966f4","full_token_estimate":746,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"docs/gap_analysis.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":293,"byte_count":14276,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":20,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":38,"role":"Assistant","content":"Let me search for the actual Osterwalder-Seiler / Lüscher proof to understand the correct mathematical argument.","timestamp":1784915405,"token_count":31,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"query\":\"Osterwalder Seiler lattice gauge theory reflection positivity character expansion\",\"source\":\"web\",\"max_results\":5}","name":"search_literature"},"id":"a7d2d9cf-e04f-47cc-bfe6-126002b494a3","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"query\":\"Luscher transfer matrix lattice gauge theory Osterwalder-Schrader positivity Wilson action\",\"source\":\"web\",\"max_results\":5}","name":"search_literature"},"id":"78c2b391-2faa-4f28-81ec-1c026c2cfcc2","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":1007,"reasoning_content":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the situation. Let me search for the actual Osterwalder-Seiler / Lüscher proof to understand the correct mathematical argument.\n\nThe key issue is: the gap_analysis says the character expansion decomposes `exp(c·Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃g₄))` into `∑ χ_μ(g₁)·χ_ν(g₂)·χ_ρ(g₃)·χ_σ(g₄)` with non-negative coefficients. But I showed that `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is NOT PD on `G⁴` (counterexample with SU(2)).\n\nSo either:\n1. The decomposition `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃g₄) = ∑ C_{μνρσ} χ_μ(g₁)χ_ν(g₂)χ_ρ(g₃)χ_σ(g₄)` with `C_{μνρσ} ≥ 0` is FALSE (which my counterexample suggests), or\n2. The correct argument is different from what the gap_analysis describes.\n\nLet me search for the Lüscher proof, which is described as an \"alternative proof using explicit transfer matrix construction\" that \"does not require fermions.\"\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The gap_analysis says the decomposition is for `exp(c·Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃g₄))` — note it says `g₁g₂g₃g₄`, not `g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹`. But the plaquette is `g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹` (with inverses). Maybe the inverses matter?\n\nActually, the character expansion is `exp(c·Re Tr(g)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g)`. This is for a SINGLE group element `g`. For the plaquette, `g = g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹`, so:\n\n`exp(c·Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)) = ∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`.\n\nNow, the claim is that `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` can be decomposed as `∑ C_{μνρσ} χ_μ(g₁)χ_ν(g₂)χ_ρ(g₃)χ_σ(g₄)` with `C_{μνρσ} ≥ 0`.\n\nBut I showed `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is NOT PD on `G⁴` (counterexample), while `∑ C_{μνρσ} χ_μ(g₁)χ_ν(g₂)χ_ρ(g₃)χ_σ(g₄)` with `C ≥ 0` WOULD be PD (product of PD functions, sum with non-negative coefficients). Contradiction!\n\nSo the decomposition with non-negative coefficients is FALSE. The gap_analysis is incorrect about this.\n\nLet me search for the actual proof to understand what's really going on.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider my counterexample. I showed that `Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` does NOT satisfy `F(g⁻¹) = conj(F(g))`, which is a necessary condition for PD. But let me double-check this.\n\n`F(g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄) = Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)`.\n`F((g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄)⁻¹) = F(g₁⁻¹,g₂⁻¹,g₃⁻¹,g₄⁻¹) = Tr(g₁⁻¹g₂⁻¹(g₃⁻¹)⁻¹(g₄⁻¹)⁻¹) = Tr(g₁⁻¹g₂⁻¹g₃g₄)`.\n\n`conj(F(g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄)) = conj(Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)) = Tr((g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)ᴴ) = Tr(g₄⁻¹ᴴg₃⁻¹ᴴg₂ᴴg₁ᴴ) = Tr(g₄g₃g₂⁻¹g₁⁻¹)`.\n\nUsing cyclic property: `Tr(g₄g₃g₂⁻¹g₁⁻¹) = Tr(g₁⁻¹g₄g₃g₂⁻¹) = Tr(g₂⁻¹g₁⁻¹g₄g₃) = ...`.\n\nAnd `F(g₁⁻¹,g₂⁻¹,g₃⁻¹,g₄⁻¹) = Tr(g₁⁻¹g₂⁻¹g₃g₄)`.\n\nSo `conj(F(g)) = Tr(g₄g₃g₂⁻¹g₁⁻¹)` and `F(g⁻¹) = Tr(g₁⁻¹g₂⁻¹g₃g₄)`.\n\nUsing cyclic property on `conj(F(g))`: `Tr(g₄g₃g₂⁻¹g₁⁻¹) = Tr(g₁⁻¹g₄g₃g₂⁻¹)`. This is NOT equal to `Tr(g₁⁻¹g₂⁻¹g₃g₄)` in general (the order of `g₂⁻¹, g₃, g₄` is different).\n\nSo indeed `F(g⁻¹) ≠ conj(F(g))` in general, confirming that `Tr(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is NOT PD on `G⁴`.\n\nNow, the character `χ_λ` IS a class function, so `χ_λ(AB) = χ_λ(BA)`. This means:\n`χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹) = χ_λ(g₄⁻¹g₁g₂g₃⁻¹) = χ_λ(g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹g₁g₂) = ...` (cyclic permutations).\n\nAnd `conj(χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)) = χ_λ((g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)⁻¹) = χ_λ(g₄g₃g₂⁻¹g₁⁻¹)` (using `conj(χ(g)) = χ(g⁻¹)` for unitary reps).\n\nSo `F(g⁻¹) = χ_λ(g₁⁻¹g₂⁻¹g₃g₄)` and `conj(F(g)) = χ_λ(g₄g₃g₂⁻¹g₁⁻¹)`.\n\nUsing cyclic property: `χ_λ(g₄g₃g₂⁻¹g₁⁻¹) = χ_λ(g₁⁻¹g₄g₃g₂⁻¹)`. And `F(g⁻¹) = χ_λ(g₁⁻¹g₂⁻¹g₃g₄)`.\n\nThese are NOT equal in general (different order of `g₂⁻¹, g₃, g₄`).\n\nSo even for a character `χ_λ`, `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃⁻¹g₄⁻¹)` is NOT PD on `G⁴`!\n\nThis definitively shows that the approach described in the gap_analysis (decomposing into products of characters with non-negative coefficients) is mathematically incorrect.\n\nSo what IS the correct Osterwalder-Seiler / Lüscher proof?\n\nLet me search for it.","turn":22,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":39,"role":"Tool","content":"---\nTitle: Heavy-Quark Masses from the Fermilab Method in Three-Flavor Lattice QCD\nAuthors: Elizabeth D. Freeland; Andreas S. Kronfeld; James N. Simone; Ruth S. Van de Water; Fermilab Lattice; MILC Collaborations\nAbstract: We report on heavy quark mass calculations using Fermilab heavy quarks. Lattice calculations of heavy-strange meson masses are combined with one-loop (automated) lattice perturbation theory to arrive at the quark mass. Mesons are constructed from Fermilab heavy quarks and staggered light quarks. We ...\n\n---\nTitle: Form factors for $B$ to $Kll$ semileptonic decay from three-flavor lattice QCD\nAuthors: Ran Zhou; Jon A. Bailey; Alexei Bazavov; Aida X. El-Khadra; Steven Gottlieb; Rajendra D. Jain; Andreas S. Kronfeld; Ruth S. Van de Water; Fermilab Lattice; MILC Collaborations\nAbstract: We study the $B \\to Kl^+l^-$ semileptonic decay process in three-flavor lattice QCD. We analyze several ensembles generated by the MILC collaboration at different lattice spacings and sea-quark masses. We use the asqtad improved staggered action for the light quarks and the clover action with the Fe...\n\n---\nTitle: Scalar lattice gauge theory\nAuthors: C. Wetterich\nAbstract: Scalar lattice gauge theories are models for scalar fields with local gauge symmetries. No fundamental gauge fields, or link variables in a lattice regularization, are introduced. The latter rather emerge as collective excitations composed from scalars. For suitable parameters scalar lattice gauge t...\n\n---\nTitle: Recent progress in lattice supersymmetry: from lattice gauge theory to black holes\nAuthors: Daisuke Kadoh\nAbstract: Supersymmetry (SUSY) is a fascinating topic in theoretical physics, because of its unique and counterintuitive properties. It is expected to emerge as new physics beyond the standard model, and it is also a building block for supergravity and superstring theory. A number of exact results obtained vi...\n\n---\nTitle: Applications of Jarzynski's relation in lattice gauge theories\nAuthors: Alessandro Nada; Michele Caselle; Gianluca Costagliola; Marco Panero; Arianna Toniato\nAbstract: Jarzynski's equality is a well-known result in statistical mechanics, relating free-energy differences between equilibrium ensembles with fluctuations in the work performed during non-equilibrium transformations from one ensemble to the other. In this work, an extension of this relation to lattice g...\n","timestamp":1784915407,"token_count":606,"tool_call_id":"a7d2d9cf-e04f-47cc-bfe6-126002b494a3","full_token_estimate":563,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"search_literature","file_path":null,"old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":null,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":919,"edit_line":null},"turn":22,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":40,"role":"Tool","content":"---\nTitle: Form factors for $B$ to $Kll$ semileptonic decay from three-flavor lattice QCD\nAuthors: Ran Zhou; Jon A. Bailey; Alexei Bazavov; Aida X. El-Khadra; Steven Gottlieb; Rajendra D. Jain; Andreas S. Kronfeld; Ruth S. Van de Water; Fermilab Lattice; MILC Collaborations\nAbstract: We study the $B \\to Kl^+l^-$ semileptonic decay process in three-flavor lattice QCD. We analyze several ensembles generated by the MILC collaboration at different lattice spacings and sea-quark masses. We use the asqtad improved staggered action for the light quarks and the clover action with the Fe...\n\n---\nTitle: Heavy-Quark Masses from the Fermilab Method in Three-Flavor Lattice QCD\nAuthors: Elizabeth D. Freeland; Andreas S. Kronfeld; James N. Simone; Ruth S. Van de Water; Fermilab Lattice; MILC Collaborations\nAbstract: We report on heavy quark mass calculations using Fermilab heavy quarks. Lattice calculations of heavy-strange meson masses are combined with one-loop (automated) lattice perturbation theory to arrive at the quark mass. Mesons are constructed from Fermilab heavy quarks and staggered light quarks. We ...\n\n---\nTitle: Chiral gauge theories on the lattice with exact gauge invariance\nAuthors: Martin Lüscher\nAbstract: A recently proposed formulation of chiral lattice gauge theories is reviewed, in which the locality and gauge invariance of the theory can be preserved if the fermion representation of the gauge group is anomaly-free....\n\n---\nTitle: Scalar lattice gauge theory\nAuthors: C. Wetterich\nAbstract: Scalar lattice gauge theories are models for scalar fields with local gauge symmetries. No fundamental gauge fields, or link variables in a lattice regularization, are introduced. The latter rather emerge as collective excitations composed from scalars. For suitable parameters scalar lattice gauge t...\n\n---\nTitle: Recent progress in lattice supersymmetry: from lattice gauge theory to black holes\nAuthors: Daisuke Kadoh\nAbstract: Supersymmetry (SUSY) is a fascinating topic in theoretical physics, because of its unique and counterintuitive properties. It is expected to emerge as new physics beyond the standard model, and it is also a building block for supergravity and superstring theory. A number of exact results obtained vi...\n","timestamp":1784915407,"token_count":568,"tool_call_id":"78c2b391-2faa-4f28-81ec-1c026c2cfcc2","full_token_estimate":529,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"search_literature","file_path":null,"old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":null,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":1348,"edit_line":null},"turn":22,"is_prune_marker":false}
