μ(g₁) χ_ν(g₂) χ_ρ(g₃) χ_σ(g₄)`\n- Each `χ_μ(g₁) χ_ν(g₂) χ_ρ(g₃) χ_σ(g₄)` is PD on `SU(N)^4` (by `PositiveDefinite.prod` applied 3 times, using `repCharacter_positiveDefinite` for each factor)\n- The sum with non-negative coefficients is PD (by `PositiveDefinite.add` and `PositiveDefinite.smul_nonneg`)\n\n## Key files to read FIRST (in this order)\n\n1. **`src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/PositiveDefinite.lean`** — ALL PD infrastructure, fully proven, 0 sorries. Critical lemmas:\n   - `PositiveDefinite` def (line 36): `∀ s c, ∑∑ c_i conj(c_j) φ(i⁻¹*j) ≥ 0`\n   - `PositiveDefinite.add` (line 44), `PositiveDefinite.smul_nonneg` (line 56), `PositiveDefinite.zero`, `PositiveDefinite.conj` (line 474), `PositiveDefinite.pow`, `PositiveDefinite.tendsto`\n   - `PositiveDefinite.prod` (line ~400, in `section ProductGroup`): if φ:G→ℂ PD and ψ:H→ℂ PD then (g,h)↦φ(g)*ψ(h) PD on G×H. Uses `matrix_posSemidef_of_map` + Schur product theorem.\n   - `reTrace_positiveDefinite` (line 492): `(g ↦ (Re Tr g))` is PD on SU(N)\n   - `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite` (line 510): `exp(c·Re Tr g)` is PD on SU(N) for c≥0 (via power series + `tendsto`)\n   - `repCharacter_positiveDefinite` (line 580, in `section UnitaryRepresentation`): character of any unitary rep is PD. Uses `IsUnitaryRepresentation`, `repCharacter`, `conjTranspose_eq_inv_of_unitary`.\n   - File ends at line 641. Namespace: `YangMills` (no inner namespace). Imports: Mathlib analysis/matrix stuff + `YangMills.SpecialUnitary`.\n\n2. **`docs/found_issues.md` §3** (lines 115-214) — the precise mathematical obstruction. KEY facts:\n   - `Tr(gh)` is NOT PD on SU(N)×SU(N) (Pauli matrix counterexample, eigenvalues -2,4,4)\n   - The multiplication map SU(N)^k → SU(N) is NOT a group homomorphism for non-abelian groups, so composing PD with it does NOT preserve PD\n   - The character expansion resolves this: decomposes `exp(c·Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃g₄))` into sum of products `χ_μ(g₁)χ_ν(g₂)χ_ρ(g₃)χ_σ(g₄)` with non-neg coefficients (Littlewood-Richardson), each factor PD on its own SU(N), product PD on SU(N)^4 by `PositiveDefinite.prod`.\n\n3. **`docs/gap_analysis.md`** (lines 41-145) — status of all components. The \"Mathematical Proof (Osterwalder-Seiler 1979, §3)\" section (lines 79-128) gives the 5-step proof. References: Osterwalder-Seiler 1978, Lüscher 1977/1987, Menotti-Pelissetto 1987.\n\n4. **`src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean` lines 1331-1437** — the sorry. The goal at line 1413-1437 is `_ ≥ 0` where the LHS is `∫ G(U_ext cfg) * G(reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) ∂ μ₀`. The `sorry` is at line 1437. The surrounding `calc` (lines 1407-1437) already rewrites the integrand to `G(U_ext cfg) * G(reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg))`. Imports: `YangMills.Proofs.LatticeMeasure`, `YangMills.Proofs.BasicLemmas`. Namespace: `YangMills.Lattice` inside `section PeriodicSite` (line 147). `set_option maxHeartbeats 2000000`.\n\n5. **`src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean`** — the transfer matrix framework, fully proven (0 sorries). Key:\n   - `transferMatrixCorrect` (line 1186): `(T ψ)(u) = ∫ ψ(reflectToPosInterface ...) * exp(-β*(...)) ∂ haarMeasureNegative`\n   - `g_posInterface` (line 1234): `g(u) = f(u)·exp(-β·osPositiveOfPosInterface/2)`\n   - `G` (line 1214): `G(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2)`\n   - `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` (line 2466): PROVEN. `∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g(u)·(Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰`. This is the measure-theoretic identity. The remaining step is `∫ g(u)·(Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰ ≥ 0`, which requires T to be a positive operator, which requires the plaquette kernel to be PD, which requires Peter-Weyl.\n   - Imports `YangMills.Proofs.ReflectionPositivity`. Namespace `YangMills.Lattice` inside `section TransferMatrix` (line 167). `variable (N T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T) (β : ℝ)` (line 171).\n\n6. **`src/lean/YangMills/Lattice.lean`** — core definitions:\n   - `LinkVariable` (line 172), `reflectLinkVariable` (line 187), `plaquetteProduct` (line 239): `U.value n μ * U.value (n+μ) ν * (U.value (n+μ+ν) μ)⁻¹ * (U.value (n+ν) ν)⁻¹`\n   - `plaquetteContribution` (line 255): `β*(1 - (1/N)*Re Tr(plaquetteProduct))`\n   - `wilsonActionFinite` (line 270): sum over sites and μ,ν of plaquetteContribution\n\n## Design decisions for PeterWeyl.lean\n\n- **Use axioms** for Peter-Weyl and Clebsch-Gordan (these are deep theorems not in Mathlib). Clearly document them as axiomatized. The Osterwalder-Seiler proof is the mathematical justification.\n- The character expansion axiom should be stated for `exp(c · Re Tr(g))` on SU(N) with `c ≥ 0`, giving a sum over representations with non-negative coefficients. Since Lean can't quantify over \"all irreps of SU(N)\" easily, consider a finite-indexed family of unitary representations `ρ_λ : SU N →* Matrix (Fin n_λ) (Fin n_λ) ℂ` with `IsUnitaryRepresentation`, and axiomatize that `exp(c·Re Tr g) = ∑_λ a_λ * repCharacter ρ_λ g` with `a_λ ≥ 0`.\n- The Clebsch-Gordan axiom: for each `ρ_λ`, `repCharacter ρ_λ (g*h) = ∑_{μ,ν} N^λ_{μν} * repCharacter ρ_μ g * repCharacter ρ_ν h` with `N^λ_{μν} ≥ 0`. (Apply iteratively for 4 factors.)\n- Then prove `plaquetteBoltzmannPD : PositiveDefinite (λ (g₁,g₂,g₃,g₄ : SU N) => Real.exp (c * (Matrix.trace (g₁*g₂*g₃*g₄)).re))` for `c ≥ 0` — wait, this is the composition issue. Actually the PD is on SU(N)^4 of the function `exp(c·Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃g₄))`. By character expansion this equals `∑_λ a_λ χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃g₄)`, and by Clebsch-Gordan `χ_λ(g₁g₂g₃g₄) = ∑ N χ_μ(g₁)χ_ν(g₂)χ_ρ(g₃)χ_σ(g₄)`, each product PD on SU(N)^4 by `PositiveDefinite.prod` (3x) + `repCharacter_positiveDefinite`, sum PD by `add`/`smul_nonneg`.\n- Then prove the transfer matrix positivity: the kernel of `transferMatrixCorrect` is a product of such plaquette Boltzmann factors (over interface plaquettes), hence PD, hence T positive, hence `∫ g·(Tg) ≥ 0`.\n- Finally wire into ReflectionPositivity.lean:1437. The `calc` at line 1407 already has the integrand as `G(U_ext cfg) * G(reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg))`. Use `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` (from TransferMatrix.lean, which imports ReflectionPositivity — so you may need to put the final wiring in TransferMatrix.lean or restructure imports to avoid a cycle) to rewrite to `∫ g·(Tg)`, then apply transfer matrix positivity.\n\n## Import cycle warning\n\nTransferMatrix.lean imports ReflectionPositivity.lean. If PeterWeyl.lean needs to use `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` AND be used by ReflectionPositivity.lean, there's a cycle. Options:\n- Put the final sorry-closing proof in TransferMatrix.lean (which already imports ReflectionPositivity and has the transfer matrix defs), adding a lemma there that proves `∫ G·G(θU) ≥ 0` using `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` + PeterWeyl positivity, then call THAT lemma from ReflectionPositivity.lean:1437. But ReflectionPositivity doesn't import TransferMatrix currently... check this.\n- OR: PeterWeyl.lean imports PositiveDefinite.lean only. TransferMatrix.lean imports PeterWeyl.lean and proves the nonneg lemma. ReflectionPositivity.lean imports TransferMatrix.lean (add this import) and uses the lemma at line 1437.\n\nVerify the import graph before writing. Run `grep import src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/*.lean` to see current imports.\n\n## Build commands\n- Single file: `lake env lean src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/PeterWeyl.lean`\n- Full build: `lake build` (reports all sorries; currently only ReflectionPositivity.lean:1331)\n- **PowerShell mangles UTF-8**: pipe `lake` output to a file then use `findstr /n /c:\"error\" /c:\"sorry\" /c:\"declaration uses\"` to read results.\n\n## Project task list status\n- Phase 6 (TransferMatrix): COMPLETED\n- PositiveDefinite.lean: COMPLETED (all PD infrastructure proven)\n- Phase 8 (Peter-Weyl): IN PROGRESS — this is your task\n- Phase 7 (continuum/mass gap): PENDING (not yet started, do after Phase 8)\n- The sorry at ReflectionPositivity.lean:1437: PENDING (blocked on Phase 8)\n\n## Important conventions\n- Match the style in PositiveDefinite.lean: `namespace YangMills`, `section`/`end` grouping, doc comments, `open Finset/Complex/Filter/Matrix`.\n- `set_option linter.unusedVariables false` is used in ReflectionPositivity.lean.\n- No dead code, no unused imports, handle errors, keep buildable after every step.\n- The `PositiveDefinite` predicate uses `Group G` and the sum `∑ i ∈ s, ∑ j ∈ s, c i * conj (c j) * φ (i⁻¹ * j) ≥ 0`. SU(N) has a `Group` instance (verify in SpecialUnitary.lean).\n\nStart by reading PositiveDefinite.lean fully (it's only 641 lines), then docs/found_issues.md §3, then create PeterWeyl.lean.","timestamp":1784918158,"token_count":3259,"tool_call_id":"64337588-3d77-4012-9346-1bd612f495a7","full_token_estimate":2361,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"handoff","file_path":null,"old_text":"Context window approaching limit (66%). This session was spent reading and understanding the project state to prepare for creating PeterWeyl.lean. No code was written this session. A clean handoff is needed so the next session can immediately begin creating PeterWeyl.lean.","new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":null,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":44,"is_prune_marker":false}
